    <div type="book" osisID="Matt" canonical="true">
        <chapter sID="Matt.1" osisID="Matt.1" n="1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.1" osisID="Matt.1.1" n="1"/>The scroll of the birth of Yeshua the Messiah, the son of David, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="4" osisRef="Matt.1.1" osisID="Matt.1.1!note.4">The pronoun for “he” or “it” is an added pronoun as it is included in the word ענה (ana, “he/it answered).. It is possible the author intended to make it present tense.</note>the son of Avraham (Abraham).<verse eID="Matt.1.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.2" osisID="Matt.1.2" n="2"/>Abraham begat Yitzhaq (Isaac), and Yitzhaq (Isaac) begat Ya’akov (Jacob); and Ya'akov (Jacob), Yehudah (Judah), and his brothers.<verse eID="Matt.1.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.3" osisID="Matt.1.3" n="3"/>Yehudah (Judah) begah Peretz (Perez) and Zerah from Tamar; Peretz begat Hetsron (Hezron), and Hetsron, Aram (Ram).<verse eID="Matt.1.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.4" osisID="Matt.1.4" n="4"/>Aram (Ram) begat Aminadab (Amminadab); and Aminadab (Amminadab), Nahshon; and Nahshon, Salmon.<verse eID="Matt.1.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.5" osisID="Matt.1.5" n="5"/>And Salmon begat Boaz from Rahab, and Boaz begat Obed from Ruth, and Obed begat Yishai (Jesse).<verse eID="Matt.1.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.6" osisID="Matt.1.6" n="6"/>Yishai (Jesse) begat David; David, Shelomoh (Solomon), from the wife of Uriyyah (Uriah).<verse eID="Matt.1.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.7" osisID="Matt.1.7" n="7"/>Shlomoh (Solomon) begat Rehabam (Rehoboam); and Rehabam begat Abiyyah (Abijah); Abiyyah, Asa.<verse eID="Matt.1.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.8" osisID="Matt.1.8" n="8"/>Asa begat Yehosaphat (Jehoshaphat); Yehoshaphat, Yoram (Joram), and Yoram, Uzziah.<verse eID="Matt.1.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.9" osisID="Matt.1.9" n="9"/>Uzziyyahu (Uzziah) begat Yotham (Jotham), Yotham begat Ahaz, Hezekiah.<verse eID="Matt.1.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.10" osisID="Matt.1.10" n="10"/>Hezqiyyah (Hezekiah) begat Menasheh (Manasseh); Menasheh, Amon; Amon, Yoshiyah (Josiah).<verse eID="Matt.1.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.11" osisID="Matt.1.11" n="11"/>Yoshiyah (Josiah) begat Yekonyah (Jeconiah) and his brothers in the exile of Babel.<verse eID="Matt.1.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.12" osisID="Matt.1.12" n="12"/>From the exile of Babel, Yekonya (Joconiah) begat She’alti’el (Shealtiel); She’alti’el, Zerubbabel.<verse eID="Matt.1.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.13" osisID="Matt.1.13" n="13"/>And Zerubbabel begat Abihud; and Abihud, Elyaqum (Eliakim); and Elyaqum begat Azor.<verse eID="Matt.1.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.14" osisID="Matt.1.14" n="14"/>Azor begat Zadok; and Zadok, Akhin (Achim); and Achin (Achim), Eliyyud (Elihud).<verse eID="Matt.1.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.15" osisID="Matt.1.15" n="15"/>Eliyyud (Elihud) begat Eleazar; Eleazar, Mattan; and Mattan, Ya’akov (Jacob).<verse eID="Matt.1.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.16" osisID="Matt.1.16" n="16"/>And Ya’akov (Jacob) begat Yoseph (Joseph), the man (i.e., “husband”) <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="9" osisRef="Matt.1.16" osisID="Matt.1.16!note.9">Or may also mean, “against you.” It also appears that the scribe may not have intended to write the word אני (ani, “I”), with the middle letter being the letter nun, but instead, he may have intended to write the word אוי (oy, “Woe!), with the middle letter being a vav. Thus, if</note>of Miriam (Mary), from whom was born Yeshua, who is called the Messiah.<verse eID="Matt.1.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.17" osisID="Matt.1.17" n="17"/>All this, from Abraham until David’s kindred, was fourteen, and from David until the exile of Babel (Babylon), fourteen generations, and from the exile of Babel until Messiah, fourteen generations.<verse eID="Matt.1.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.18" osisID="Matt.1.18" n="18"/>The birth of Yeshua Messiah was accordingly: when his mother, Miriam (Mary) the betrothed of Yoseph (Joseph), though not yet been joined together, she was found to be pregnant from the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit).<verse eID="Matt.1.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.19" osisID="Matt.1.19" n="19"/>And Yoseph (Joseph), this husband of hers, was righteous and did not want to publicize, but to pardon and hide, and cause to leave.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="13" osisRef="Matt.1.19" osisID="Matt.1.19!note.13">This is a harsh word that can mean “whore, fornicator,” or “prostitute.” The proper word for “adulterer” is נואף (no’ef).</note> Alternate #<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="2" osisRef="Matt.1.19" osisID="Matt.1.19!note.2">Weinstein says, “In Oo.1.32—a complete NT save for Revelation—we distinguish the handwritings of no fewer than three copyists”: M. M. Weinstein, “A Hebrew Qur’an Manuscript,” Studies in Bibliography and Booklore 10 (Winter 1971/72): 35</note> Translation: And Yoseph (Joseph), this husband of hers, was righteous and did not want to publicize, but pardon her to hide and to leave.<verse eID="Matt.1.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.20" osisID="Matt.1.20" n="20"/>When he had this intent, the messenger of MarYah (Master Yehovah) became visible to him in a dream and said to him, “Son of David, do not fear to take Mirayam (Mary), your wife, for she is pregnant from the Holy Spirit.<verse eID="Matt.1.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.21" osisID="Matt.1.21" n="21"/>She bears this son, and you will call His name, Yeshua, for He redeems <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="16" osisRef="Matt.1.21" osisID="Matt.1.21!note.16">In Matthew 16:19, Yeshua is speaking directly to Peter about building an assembly upon the stone of truth that Yeshua is the Messiah by saying, “To you I will give the keys of heaven and all of what you prohibit ( אוסר oser) in the world will be prohibited in the heavens, and all of what remains will be permitted in the heaven.” However, in Matthew 18:18, Yeshua is speaking directly to all the disciples about handling conflict in the community by saying, “...all what is bound ( קשור kishur) on earth, is bound in the heavens: and what is opened on earth will be opened in the heavens.” These are speaking of two different truths: 1. The truth that Yeshua is the Messiah is the foundation of the new covenant assembly. However, if “the heavens” is an idiom of “heavenly beings,” according to Joseph A. Fitzmeyer’s analysis of the 1350 Hittite treaty (See the English Edition of the Aramaic text, with references to biblical parallels, in Joseph A. Fitzmyer, trans., “The Inscriptions of Bar-Ga’yah and Mati’el from Sefire,” The Context of Scripture 2.82 (W. Hallo and K. L. Younger, eds. [Leiden: Brill, 2000]), 213–215), then the keys to the heavens appear to be keys that permit or prohibit heavenly beings in situations such as when an individual is turned over the satan for destruction of the flesh and correction when Paul turned two individuals over to Satan in 1 Corinthians 5:5, and 1 Timothy 1:20. Even though Yeshua was speaking directly to Peter, and Paul, who was not a disciple of Yeshua but was sent by Yeshua, was given LEGAL AUTHORITY over the assemblies was able to turn these men over to Satan. This can also be done by those who have legal authority over the assembly, but it is recommended that you ask Yehovah to fight the fight on their behalf, other than attempting to face off with demonic entities. 2. In Matthew 18:18, the disciples are taught how to resolve conflicts and to be humble with a forgiving heart. This verse seems to indicate that “all what is bound,” meaning either by man or God, is bound in the heavens. Likewise, all that is opened on earth, by man or God, is opened in the heavens. Therefore, if a person “binds” something on earth, such as a spiritual being, then it is bound in the heavens. But if something is “opened” on earth — like a modern television, radio, internet, or a cursed item — then it is opened upon the</note>the people from sins,<verse eID="Matt.1.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.22" osisID="Matt.1.22" n="22"/>This was from what was said of the prophet:<verse eID="Matt.1.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.23" osisID="Matt.1.23" n="23"/>“Behold! The maiden <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="18" osisRef="Matt.1.23" osisID="Matt.1.23!note.18">The Nit’pael verb binyan is a mix of the Nif’al and Hit’pael verb binyanim and was used regularly in Mishnaic Hebrew to express a</note>is pregnant and bears <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="19" osisRef="Matt.1.23" osisID="Matt.1.23!note.19">This compelling verse highlights that Yeshua urges us: if we seek eternal life, we must follow His commandments. See Leviticus 18:5, Ezekiel</note>a son, and you shall call His name,” Immanu’el,” which translates to, “Our God is with us.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="20" osisRef="Matt.1.23" osisID="Matt.1.23!note.20">This word is first found in Mishnah Avodah Zara 5:3.</note><verse eID="Matt.1.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.24" osisID="Matt.1.24" n="24"/>And when he rose from his sleep, he did as the messenger of the MarYah (Master Yehovah) ordered him, and he lifted himself up, and his wife.<verse eID="Matt.1.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.1.25" osisID="Matt.1.25" n="25"/>And he did not know her <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="24" osisRef="Matt.1.25" osisID="Matt.1.25!note.24">Literally, “If it is you, command that I, he will come…” Thus,</note>until she bore her firstborn son, and he called his name Yeshua.<verse eID="Matt.1.25"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.1"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.2" osisID="Matt.2" n="2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.1" osisID="Matt.2.1" n="1"/>When Yeshua was born in Beit Lechem (Bethlehem) of Yehudah (Judah) in the days of Horodos (Herodes), the king, magicians from the East came to Yerushalem (Jerusalem),<verse eID="Matt.2.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.2" osisID="Matt.2.2" n="2"/>and they said, “Now the king of the Yehudim (Jews) <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="4" osisRef="Matt.2.2" osisID="Matt.2.2!note.4">The pronoun for “he” or “it” is an added pronoun as it is included in the word ענה (ana, “he/it answered).. It is possible the author intended to make it present tense.</note>is born,” for they saw a star of the east, they came to bow down to Him.<verse eID="Matt.2.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.3" osisID="Matt.2.3" n="3"/>And Horodos (Herodes) the king, heard this and trembled a̶nd all Yerushalayim (Jerusalem) with him.<verse eID="Matt.2.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.4" osisID="Matt.2.4" n="4"/>And he gathered all the chief priests and scribes of the people, and asked them, “Where is the Messiah born?”<verse eID="Matt.2.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.5" osisID="Matt.2.5" n="5"/>They said, “In Beit Lechem (Bethlehem) of Yehudah (Judah), according to the writing of the prophet.<verse eID="Matt.2.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.6" osisID="Matt.2.6" n="6"/>And you, Beit Lechem (Bethlehem) of Yehudah (Judah), were not young from the thousands of Yehudah; from you to me will go out a king, an approved one<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="7" osisRef="Matt.2.6" osisID="Matt.2.6!note.7">See Exodus 21:17 and Leviticus 20:9.</note> for my people Israel.<verse eID="Matt.2.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.7" osisID="Matt.2.7" n="7"/>So now Horodes (Herodes) called the magicians and the sages <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="4" osisRef="Matt.2.7" osisID="Matt.2.7!note.4">The pronoun for “he” or “it” is an added pronoun as it is included in the word ענה (ana, “he/it answered).. It is possible the author intended to make it present tense.</note>and learned from them what time the star became visible.<verse eID="Matt.2.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.8" osisID="Matt.2.8" n="8"/>And he sent them to Beit Lechem (Bethlehem) and said to them, “Go in secrecy because of the boy, who was born, and when you have found him, come, and you tell me, and also I will go and bow down to Him.”<verse eID="Matt.2.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.9" osisID="Matt.2.9" n="9"/>When they heard from the king, they went, and this star that they saw in the east went before them, until they came, and it stood up to the place where the boy was.<verse eID="Matt.2.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.10" osisID="Matt.2.10" n="10"/>When they saw the stars, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="8" osisRef="Matt.2.10" osisID="Matt.2.10!note.8">This word for “divorce” ( יגרש - yegaresh) describes one who “drives out, thrusts out, or expels” his wife.</note>they rejoiced with great and delightful joy.<verse eID="Matt.2.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.11" osisID="Matt.2.11" n="11"/>And they entered into the house and saw the boy with his mother, Miryam (Mary), and they bowed down to Him and opened their treasures and offered to Him an offering of gold and musk and frankincense.<verse eID="Matt.2.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.12" osisID="Matt.2.12" n="12"/>And it appeared to them in a dream that they should not return to Horodos (Herodes), and they went a different path to their place.<verse eID="Matt.2.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.13" osisID="Matt.2.13" n="13"/>When they went, in a dream appeared the MarYah (Master Yehovah) to Yoseph and said, “Rise, take the boy and His mother and escape to Mitzrayim (Egypt), and there you shall remain until I tell you, for Horodos (Herodes) wants to kill the boy.”<verse eID="Matt.2.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.14" osisID="Matt.2.14" n="14"/>This Yoseph (Joseph) rose and took the boy and His mother at night and escaped to and was there until he made it to Mitzrayim (Egypt).<verse eID="Matt.2.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.15" osisID="Matt.2.15" n="15"/>And he remained there until Horodos (Herodes) died to complete what was spoken by the MarYah (Master Yehovah) to the prophet who said, “And from Mitzrayim (Egypt) I have called my Son.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="9" osisRef="Matt.2.15" osisID="Matt.2.15!note.9">Or may also mean, “against you.” It also appears that the scribe may not have intended to write the word אני (ani, “I”), with the middle letter being the letter nun, but instead, he may have intended to write the word אוי (oy, “Woe!), with the middle letter being a vav. Thus, if</note><verse eID="Matt.2.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.16" osisID="Matt.2.16" n="16"/>And now, when Horodos (Herodes) saw that he was degraded by the magicians, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="11" osisRef="Matt.2.16" osisID="Matt.2.16!note.11">In Deuteronomy 24:1, the Hebrew word used for “writing” is סֵפֶר (sefer), which means, “book, scroll, writing.” However, in this verse, the author</note>he schemed much and sent/ordered to kill all the boys of Bet Lehem (Bethlehem) in all its region, from two years and under, according to when it was declared to him by the magicians.<verse eID="Matt.2.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.17" osisID="Matt.2.17" n="17"/>And now was taken place what was spoken by Yirmeyah (Jeremiah) the prophet, who said,<verse eID="Matt.2.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.18" osisID="Matt.2.18" n="18"/>“A voice in Ramah was heard, there is weeping and bitter wailings: Rakhel (Rachel) lamenting over her children, refusing to be comforted, for they are not.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="12" osisRef="Matt.2.18" osisID="Matt.2.18!note.12">Literal meanings: “drives out, thrusts, expels.”</note><verse eID="Matt.2.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.19" osisID="Matt.2.19" n="19"/>When Horodos (Herodes) the king died, a messenger of the LORD (Yehovah) appeared in a dream to Yoseph (Joseph) in Mitzrayim (Egypt) and said to him,<verse eID="Matt.2.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.20" osisID="Matt.2.20" n="20"/>“Rise, take the boy and His mother and go to the land of Yisra’el (Israel), for those seeking the soul of the boy have died.”<verse eID="Matt.2.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.21" osisID="Matt.2.21" n="21"/>And Yoseph (Joseph) rose and took the boy and His mother and came to the land of Yisrael (Israel).<verse eID="Matt.2.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.22" osisID="Matt.2.22" n="22"/>When he heard that Archeleos (Archelaos) was king in Judah instead of Horodos (Herodes), his father feared going there. However, he had a dream to go to the location of Gilil (Galilee).<verse eID="Matt.2.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.2.23" osisID="Matt.2.23" n="23"/>Then he came and dwelt in the province called Nazareth, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="14" osisRef="Matt.2.23" osisID="Matt.2.23!note.14">Collectively, including details from Matthew 5:32 and Luke 16:18, along with this passage, the man who marries the first wife will be an adulterer נואף (no’ef) because the woman was not divorced properly with her כריתות (keritut, “divorcement”). The woman who is separated, or rather “put away” גרושה (gerusha), is still considered married. The man in Matthew 19:9 makes himself a fornicator זונה (zonah) by taking a 2nd woman without</note>as that would take place what was said of the prophet, “What of whom will be called of Nazarene?”<verse eID="Matt.2.23"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.2"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.3" osisID="Matt.3" n="3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.1" osisID="Matt.3.1" n="1"/>On that day, Yochanan (John) the sinker of man (babti),,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="4" osisRef="Matt.3.1" osisID="Matt.3.1!note.4">The pronoun for “he” or “it” is an added pronoun as it is included in the word ענה (ana, “he/it answered).. It is possible the author intended to make it present tense.</note>,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="5" osisRef="Matt.3.1" osisID="Matt.3.1!note.5">Genesis 1:27.</note>,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="6" osisRef="Matt.3.1" osisID="Matt.3.1!note.6">This verse is comparable to Yehovah speaking from Heaven in Matthew 3:17, and in the Cochin Matthew 3:17, it states, “...and to him,</note> <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="7" osisRef="Matt.3.1" osisID="Matt.3.1!note.7">See Exodus 21:17 and Leviticus 20:9.</note>was proclaiming in the desert-like<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="8" osisRef="Matt.3.1" osisID="Matt.3.1!note.8">This word for “divorce” ( יגרש - yegaresh) describes one who “drives out, thrusts out, or expels” his wife.</note> region of the Yehudim<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="9" osisRef="Matt.3.1" osisID="Matt.3.1!note.9">Or may also mean, “against you.” It also appears that the scribe may not have intended to write the word אני (ani, “I”), with the middle letter being the letter nun, but instead, he may have intended to write the word אוי (oy, “Woe!), with the middle letter being a vav. Thus, if</note> (Jews) saying,<verse eID="Matt.3.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.2" osisID="Matt.3.2" n="2"/>“Return! For the kingdom of heaven is near!,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="11" osisRef="Matt.3.2" osisID="Matt.3.2!note.11">In Deuteronomy 24:1, the Hebrew word used for “writing” is סֵפֶר (sefer), which means, “book, scroll, writing.” However, in this verse, the author</note><note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="12" osisRef="Matt.3.2" osisID="Matt.3.2!note.12">Literal meanings: “drives out, thrusts, expels.”</note><verse eID="Matt.3.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.3" osisID="Matt.3.3" n="3"/>Behold! For this is who was mentioned by Isaiah the prophet, ‘A voice calling in the desert: ‘Turn to the path of <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="13" osisRef="Matt.3.3" osisID="Matt.3.3!note.13">This is a harsh word that can mean “whore, fornicator,” or “prostitute.” The proper word for “adulterer” is נואף (no’ef).</note>Yehovah, make it straight in the desert.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="14" osisRef="Matt.3.3" osisID="Matt.3.3!note.14">Collectively, including details from Matthew 5:32 and Luke 16:18, along with this passage, the man who marries the first wife will be an adulterer נואף (no’ef) because the woman was not divorced properly with her כריתות (keritut, “divorcement”). The woman who is separated, or rather “put away” גרושה (gerusha), is still considered married. The man in Matthew 19:9 makes himself a fornicator זונה (zonah) by taking a 2nd woman without</note><verse eID="Matt.3.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.4" osisID="Matt.3.4" n="4"/>This John had clothing of the skin that was of a camel, and a leather belt upon his waist, and his food was grasshoppers and honey of the forest.<verse eID="Matt.3.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.5" osisID="Matt.3.5" n="5"/>And then he went out of Yerushalayim (Jerusalem) and all the Yehudim (Jews), and all the area surrounding the Yarden (Jordan),<verse eID="Matt.3.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.6" osisID="Matt.3.6" n="6"/>they were baptized (all of them, those who were converted) from the river Yarden (Jordan), and they confessed their sin.<verse eID="Matt.3.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.7" osisID="Matt.3.7" n="7"/>As soon as they saw this, many of the sages and the Sadducees came to the one who does the conversions. He said to them, “Offspring of serpents, who told you to flee from the rage that will come?”<verse eID="Matt.3.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.8" osisID="Matt.3.8" n="8"/>“Do this like a meal of fruit which is worthy of goodwill.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="16" osisRef="Matt.3.8" osisID="Matt.3.8!note.16">In Matthew 16:19, Yeshua is speaking directly to Peter about building an assembly upon the stone of truth that Yeshua is the Messiah by saying, “To you I will give the keys of heaven and all of what you prohibit ( אוסר oser) in the world will be prohibited in the heavens, and all of what remains will be permitted in the heaven.” However, in Matthew 18:18, Yeshua is speaking directly to all the disciples about handling conflict in the community by saying, “...all what is bound ( קשור kishur) on earth, is bound in the heavens: and what is opened on earth will be opened in the heavens.” These are speaking of two different truths: 1. The truth that Yeshua is the Messiah is the foundation of the new covenant assembly. However, if “the heavens” is an idiom of “heavenly beings,” according to Joseph A. Fitzmeyer’s analysis of the 1350 Hittite treaty (See the English Edition of the Aramaic text, with references to biblical parallels, in Joseph A. Fitzmyer, trans., “The Inscriptions of Bar-Ga’yah and Mati’el from Sefire,” The Context of Scripture 2.82 (W. Hallo and K. L. Younger, eds. [Leiden: Brill, 2000]), 213–215), then the keys to the heavens appear to be keys that permit or prohibit heavenly beings in situations such as when an individual is turned over the satan for destruction of the flesh and correction when Paul turned two individuals over to Satan in 1 Corinthians 5:5, and 1 Timothy 1:20. Even though Yeshua was speaking directly to Peter, and Paul, who was not a disciple of Yeshua but was sent by Yeshua, was given LEGAL AUTHORITY over the assemblies was able to turn these men over to Satan. This can also be done by those who have legal authority over the assembly, but it is recommended that you ask Yehovah to fight the fight on their behalf, other than attempting to face off with demonic entities. 2. In Matthew 18:18, the disciples are taught how to resolve conflicts and to be humble with a forgiving heart. This verse seems to indicate that “all what is bound,” meaning either by man or God, is bound in the heavens. Likewise, all that is opened on earth, by man or God, is opened in the heavens. Therefore, if a person “binds” something on earth, such as a spiritual being, then it is bound in the heavens. But if something is “opened” on earth — like a modern television, radio, internet, or a cursed item — then it is opened upon the</note><verse eID="Matt.3.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.9" osisID="Matt.3.9" n="9"/>And do not think and say within yourselves, ‘We have a father, Abraham.’ I, I say to you here it is possible for Elohim to raise up (establish) sons of Abraham from these stones.<verse eID="Matt.3.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.10" osisID="Matt.3.10" n="10"/>This is the scraping tool that is on the root of the tree: every tree that does not produce good fruit will be cut down and given over in the fire.<verse eID="Matt.3.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.11" osisID="Matt.3.11" n="11"/>I am he, cleansing you by water to my desire. <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="19" osisRef="Matt.3.11" osisID="Matt.3.11!note.19">This compelling verse highlights that Yeshua urges us: if we seek eternal life, we must follow His commandments. See Leviticus 18:5, Ezekiel</note>But he that comes after me, He is stronger than me, and I am not equal to bear his sandals. And He purifies you by the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) and by fire.<verse eID="Matt.3.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.12" osisID="Matt.3.12" n="12"/>He has the fan <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="20" osisRef="Matt.3.12" osisID="Matt.3.12!note.20">This word is first found in Mishnah Avodah Zara 5:3.</note>in his hand and purifies his place, and the grain he brings into his storehouses, and the straw he burns with fire that is not extinguished.<verse eID="Matt.3.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.13" osisID="Matt.3.13" n="13"/>And in that hour, Yeshua came from Gailil (Galilee) to Yarden (Jordan) near Yochanan (John) to purify him.<verse eID="Matt.3.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.14" osisID="Matt.3.14" n="14"/>But Yochanan (John) ceased and said, “I need to be purified by you, and you come to me?”<verse eID="Matt.3.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.15" osisID="Matt.3.15" n="15"/>And Yeshua responded and answered and said to him, “Permit this now, for the purpose of this is well for us to establish all judgments,” and he permitted him in his sign of that hour.<verse eID="Matt.3.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.16" osisID="Matt.3.16" n="16"/>When Yeshua was purified, He hurriedly arose from the water, and the gate of the heavens opened for Him, and He saw the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) come down like a dove, and it came upon Him.<verse eID="Matt.3.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.3.17" osisID="Matt.3.17" n="17"/>Then he heard one voice of God (bat kol) from the heavens that said, “This is my beloved Son, and in Him, I desired.”<verse eID="Matt.3.17"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.3"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.4" osisID="Matt.4" n="4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.1" osisID="Matt.4.1" n="1"/>Then Yeshua was brought down to the wilderness by the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) - indeed, that He would be tested by the Satan (adversary).<verse eID="Matt.4.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.2" osisID="Matt.4.2" n="2"/>And He fasted forty days, and forty nights; then He became hungry.<verse eID="Matt.4.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.3" osisID="Matt.4.3" n="3"/>Then Satan approached Him and said to him, “If you are the son of Elohim (God), tell the stones to become bread.”<verse eID="Matt.4.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.4" osisID="Matt.4.4" n="4"/>And He responded and said, “It is written, ‘Not by bread alone will man live, for upon all that comes from the mouth of Yehovah, he will live.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="5" osisRef="Matt.4.4" osisID="Matt.4.4!note.5">Genesis 1:27.</note><verse eID="Matt.4.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.5" osisID="Matt.4.5" n="5"/>And then Satan led Him into the holy city and stood on top of the rooftop of the house of the dwelling place<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="8" osisRef="Matt.4.5" osisID="Matt.4.5!note.8">This word for “divorce” ( יגרש - yegaresh) describes one who “drives out, thrusts out, or expels” his wife.</note> and said to Him,<verse eID="Matt.4.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.6" osisID="Matt.4.6" n="6"/>“If you are the Son of Elohim (God), throw yourself down to the earth below: for according to which is written, ‘For He commands His messengers concerning you... They will bear you up in their hands, lest you dash your foot against a stone.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="9" osisRef="Matt.4.6" osisID="Matt.4.6!note.9">Or may also mean, “against you.” It also appears that the scribe may not have intended to write the word אני (ani, “I”), with the middle letter being the letter nun, but instead, he may have intended to write the word אוי (oy, “Woe!), with the middle letter being a vav. Thus, if</note><verse eID="Matt.4.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.7" osisID="Matt.4.7" n="7"/>Yeshua said to him, “But indeed, it is written, ‘You shall not try Yehovah your Elohim (God)!’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="10" osisRef="Matt.4.7" osisID="Matt.4.7!note.10">Deuteronomy 24:1.</note><verse eID="Matt.4.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.8" osisID="Matt.4.8" n="8"/>And once more, Satan took Him and raised Him up to a very high mountain, and he showed Him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory. And he said to Him,<verse eID="Matt.4.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.9" osisID="Matt.4.9" n="9"/>“All of this I will give you if you bow <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="12" osisRef="Matt.4.9" osisID="Matt.4.9!note.12">Literal meanings: “drives out, thrusts, expels.”</note>and prostrate <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="13" osisRef="Matt.4.9" osisID="Matt.4.9!note.13">This is a harsh word that can mean “whore, fornicator,” or “prostitute.” The proper word for “adulterer” is נואף (no’ef).</note>to me.”<verse eID="Matt.4.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.10" osisID="Matt.4.10" n="10"/>And then He said to him (Yeshua to Satan), “Follow after Me: <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="14" osisRef="Matt.4.10" osisID="Matt.4.10!note.14">Collectively, including details from Matthew 5:32 and Luke 16:18, along with this passage, the man who marries the first wife will be an adulterer נואף (no’ef) because the woman was not divorced properly with her כריתות (keritut, “divorcement”). The woman who is separated, or rather “put away” גרושה (gerusha), is still considered married. The man in Matthew 19:9 makes himself a fornicator זונה (zonah) by taking a 2nd woman without</note>It is written, ‘Yehovah your Elohim (God), you shall fear; and Him, you shall serve.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="15" osisRef="Matt.4.10" osisID="Matt.4.10!note.15">Can also be translated to, “men with a woman.”</note><verse eID="Matt.4.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.11" osisID="Matt.4.11" n="11"/>“Then Satan left, and lo, messengers approached and were ministering to Him.”<verse eID="Matt.4.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.12" osisID="Matt.4.12" n="12"/>And when Yeshua heard that Yochanan (John) was arrested in the house of prisoners, He returned to Galil (Galilee).<verse eID="Matt.4.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.13" osisID="Matt.4.13" n="13"/>And He left from Netzaret (Nazareth) and came to dwell in Kephar Nahum (Capernaum) beside the sea, that is in the borders of Zebulon and Naphtali,<verse eID="Matt.4.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.14" osisID="Matt.4.14" n="14"/>in order to establish the saying by the hand of the Prophet of Yeshayah (Isaiah), who said,<verse eID="Matt.4.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.15" osisID="Matt.4.15" n="15"/>“Land of Zebulun and land of Naphtali, the way of the sea across the Yarden (Jordan), Galil (Galilee) of the gentiles,”<verse eID="Matt.4.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.16" osisID="Matt.4.16" n="16"/>“the people who sit <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="16" osisRef="Matt.4.16" osisID="Matt.4.16!note.16">In Matthew 16:19, Yeshua is speaking directly to Peter about building an assembly upon the stone of truth that Yeshua is the Messiah by saying, “To you I will give the keys of heaven and all of what you prohibit ( אוסר oser) in the world will be prohibited in the heavens, and all of what remains will be permitted in the heaven.” However, in Matthew 18:18, Yeshua is speaking directly to all the disciples about handling conflict in the community by saying, “...all what is bound ( קשור kishur) on earth, is bound in the heavens: and what is opened on earth will be opened in the heavens.” These are speaking of two different truths: 1. The truth that Yeshua is the Messiah is the foundation of the new covenant assembly. However, if “the heavens” is an idiom of “heavenly beings,” according to Joseph A. Fitzmeyer’s analysis of the 1350 Hittite treaty (See the English Edition of the Aramaic text, with references to biblical parallels, in Joseph A. Fitzmyer, trans., “The Inscriptions of Bar-Ga’yah and Mati’el from Sefire,” The Context of Scripture 2.82 (W. Hallo and K. L. Younger, eds. [Leiden: Brill, 2000]), 213–215), then the keys to the heavens appear to be keys that permit or prohibit heavenly beings in situations such as when an individual is turned over the satan for destruction of the flesh and correction when Paul turned two individuals over to Satan in 1 Corinthians 5:5, and 1 Timothy 1:20. Even though Yeshua was speaking directly to Peter, and Paul, who was not a disciple of Yeshua but was sent by Yeshua, was given LEGAL AUTHORITY over the assemblies was able to turn these men over to Satan. This can also be done by those who have legal authority over the assembly, but it is recommended that you ask Yehovah to fight the fight on their behalf, other than attempting to face off with demonic entities. 2. In Matthew 18:18, the disciples are taught how to resolve conflicts and to be humble with a forgiving heart. This verse seems to indicate that “all what is bound,” meaning either by man or God, is bound in the heavens. Likewise, all that is opened on earth, by man or God, is opened in the heavens. Therefore, if a person “binds” something on earth, such as a spiritual being, then it is bound in the heavens. But if something is “opened” on earth — like a modern television, radio, internet, or a cursed item — then it is opened upon the</note>in darkness have seen the great light, and those sitting in the place of the shadow and death, a great light permeated them.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="17" osisRef="Matt.4.16" osisID="Matt.4.16!note.17">See Isaiah 6:9–10.</note><verse eID="Matt.4.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.17" osisID="Matt.4.17" n="17"/>Then He began to proclaim, saying, “Repent, for near to us is the kingdom of heaven!”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="18" osisRef="Matt.4.17" osisID="Matt.4.17!note.18">The Nit’pael verb binyan is a mix of the Nif’al and Hit’pael verb binyanim and was used regularly in Mishnaic Hebrew to express a</note><verse eID="Matt.4.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.18" osisID="Matt.4.18" n="18"/>And as Yeshua walked on the shore of the Sea of Galil (Galilee), He saw two brothers, Shim’on (Simon), named Kepha (Peter), and Andre (Andrew), his brother, casting nets into the sea, for they were fishermen.<verse eID="Matt.4.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.19" osisID="Matt.4.19" n="19"/>And He said to them, “Follow after Me. I will make you fishers of men.”<verse eID="Matt.4.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.20" osisID="Matt.4.20" n="20"/>And they left the net and went after Him.<verse eID="Matt.4.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.21" osisID="Matt.4.21" n="21"/>And when He passed from there, came two brothers: Ya’akov (Jacob/James) and Yochanan (John), his brother, in a ship with Zebdi (Zebedee), their father, improving their nets; and he called to them.<verse eID="Matt.4.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.22" osisID="Matt.4.22" n="22"/>They quickly abandoned the ship and their father and went after Him.<verse eID="Matt.4.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.23" osisID="Matt.4.23" n="23"/>And Yeshua went around all the Galileans teaching their assemblies and proclaiming the gospels of the kingdom and healing all their sick and all sicknesses in the people.<verse eID="Matt.4.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.24" osisID="Matt.4.24" n="24"/>And His report was heard in all Syria, and all approached Him who had iniquity and illness, weaknesses and pains, and even demon possession, and the deranged, and those with gout, and impeded speech, and wasted; and He healed them.<verse eID="Matt.4.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.4.25" osisID="Matt.4.25" n="25"/>Many went after Him and joined from Galil (Galilee), and from the ten cities, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="22" osisRef="Matt.4.25" osisID="Matt.4.25!note.22">Literally, “And he saw, his talmidim…”</note>and from Yerushalayim (Jerusalem), and from Yehudah (Judea), and from across the Yarden (Jordan).<verse eID="Matt.4.25"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.4"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.5" osisID="Matt.5" n="5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.1" osisID="Matt.5.1" n="1"/>When Yeshua saw a multitude of people, he ascended a mountain and sat down; the talmidim (students) came near.<verse eID="Matt.5.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.2" osisID="Matt.5.2" n="2"/>And he opened his mouth, taught them, and said,<verse eID="Matt.5.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.3" osisID="Matt.5.3" n="3"/>“Blessed are those who are poor; for their sake shall be the kingdom of heaven.”<verse eID="Matt.5.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.4" osisID="Matt.5.4" n="4"/>“Blessed are they who mourn, for they will be comforted.”<verse eID="Matt.5.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.5" osisID="Matt.5.5" n="5"/>“Blessed are the humble, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="6" osisRef="Matt.5.5" osisID="Matt.5.5!note.6">This verse is comparable to Yehovah speaking from Heaven in Matthew 3:17, and in the Cochin Matthew 3:17, it states, “...and to him,</note>for they will inherit the world.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="7" osisRef="Matt.5.5" osisID="Matt.5.5!note.7">See Exodus 21:17 and Leviticus 20:9.</note><verse eID="Matt.5.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.6" osisID="Matt.5.6" n="6"/>“Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, which they are satisfied.”<verse eID="Matt.5.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.7" osisID="Matt.5.7" n="7"/>“Blessed are those who love, for them I have compassion.”<verse eID="Matt.5.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.8" osisID="Matt.5.8" n="8"/>“Blessed are they of pure heart, for they will see Elohim (God).”<verse eID="Matt.5.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.9" osisID="Matt.5.9" n="9"/>“Blessed are those who are makers of peace, for they shall be called the children of Elohim (God).”<verse eID="Matt.5.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.10" osisID="Matt.5.10" n="10"/>“Blessed are those who labor in judgment, for they will inherit the kingdom of heaven.”<verse eID="Matt.5.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.11" osisID="Matt.5.11" n="11"/>“Blessed are you when they insult you and persecute you, saying against you (leshon ha’ra)<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="15" osisRef="Matt.5.11" osisID="Matt.5.11!note.15">Can also be translated to, “men with a woman.”</note> for the sake of my glory, to deceive.”<verse eID="Matt.5.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.12" osisID="Matt.5.12" n="12"/>“Rejoice and be happy, your reward is great in the heavens; because of me, many prophets were persecuted.”<verse eID="Matt.5.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.13" osisID="Matt.5.13" n="13"/>“You are the salt of the earth, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="20" osisRef="Matt.5.13" osisID="Matt.5.13!note.20">This word is first found in Mishnah Avodah Zara 5:3.</note>but if the salt placed in a bag <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="21" osisRef="Matt.5.13" osisID="Matt.5.13!note.21">Literally, “he/it lent.” It appears the author intended to write it as a noun, but wrote as a verb.</note>is destroyed, they throw it to the earth and isn’t advantageous, and the sons of man trample it.”<verse eID="Matt.5.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.14" osisID="Matt.5.14" n="14"/>“You are the light of the world, which is not able to hide in a high province built on a great mountain.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="22" osisRef="Matt.5.14" osisID="Matt.5.14!note.22">Literally, “And he saw, his talmidim…”</note><verse eID="Matt.5.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.15" osisID="Matt.5.15" n="15"/>“And they do not light a lamp and place it under a curtain, but they light the menorah that illuminates for all the children of the house.”<verse eID="Matt.5.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.16" osisID="Matt.5.16" n="16"/>“Therefore, shine radiantly for all the eyes of man from good deeds and praising their Father in the heavens.”<verse eID="Matt.5.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.17" osisID="Matt.5.17" n="17"/>“Do not think I came to abolish the Torah or the words of a prophet, but to establish.”,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="25" osisRef="Matt.5.17" osisID="Matt.5.17!note.25">The word קדמונים is the Hebrew equivalent of the Aramaic ܐܝܡܕܩ (qaddimāya), meaning the “first ones” from the ancient world. While accurately</note><note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="26" osisRef="Matt.5.17" osisID="Matt.5.17!note.26">Aramaic word that is a metaphor for making it rain. See Aph Dem 4.65:25: ܐܪܛܡ ܬܚܢܘ.</note><verse eID="Matt.5.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.18" osisID="Matt.5.18" n="18"/>“Although the heavens and the earth will cease, not even a letter or one dot from the Torah will cease but be established.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="29" osisRef="Matt.5.18" osisID="Matt.5.18!note.29">This word is first found in the Mishnah Tractate Sanhedrin 6:4.</note>”<verse eID="Matt.5.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.19" osisID="Matt.5.19" n="19"/>“Everyone who abolishes <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="31" osisRef="Matt.5.19" osisID="Matt.5.19!note.31">This word appears to be misspelled, and the vav is in the wrong position and should be תֹּולִים (tolim).</note>one small commandment that is in the Torah, and if he shall teach others in this way, he shall be called a little one in the kingdom of heaven; and everyone who establishes them and if he teaches others, shall be called great in the kingdom of heaven.”<verse eID="Matt.5.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.20" osisID="Matt.5.20" n="20"/>“I say to you, that they will not add in judgment more than is in the scroll or scribes, but by this, you shall be found worthy to ascend to the heavens.”<verse eID="Matt.5.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.21" osisID="Matt.5.21" n="21"/>“You heard it formerly <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="32" osisRef="Matt.5.21" osisID="Matt.5.21!note.32">Literally, “sacrifice their hand.”</note>said, “you shall not murder,” <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="33" osisRef="Matt.5.21" osisID="Matt.5.21!note.33">Literally, “indebted to.”</note>and “whoever murders, commits a penalty of death by the Beit Din (house of judgement).”<verse eID="Matt.5.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.22" osisID="Matt.5.22" n="22"/>“I say to you, everyone who is angry regarding his brother and despises him, is guilty by the Beit Din, and everyone who says regarding his brother, ‘Worthless!’ or ‘Wicked!’ is condemned to the punishment of lashes by the Beit Din. Then whoever says, ‘Madman!’ to his friend, goes down to Gehinnom.”<verse eID="Matt.5.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.23" osisID="Matt.5.23" n="23"/>“When you bring your offering, and there you remember that your brother has a matter of fault against you,”<verse eID="Matt.5.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.24" osisID="Matt.5.24" n="24"/>“then you leave your offering on the altar and go seek pardon from your brother, and afterwards, offer your offering.”<verse eID="Matt.5.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.25" osisID="Matt.5.25" n="25"/>“You shall yearn to reconcile with your accuser <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="36" osisRef="Matt.5.25" osisID="Matt.5.25!note.36">This word ( דינך ) is a legal term indicating a court of law and a legal case presented by an accuser and is usually seen in poetry. See Jeremiah 30:13.</note>while <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="37" osisRef="Matt.5.25" osisID="Matt.5.25!note.37">Literally, “while he is with him.”</note>with him on the way, before he delivers you to the judge, and the judge delivers you to the officer, and you fall into the house of prisoners.”<verse eID="Matt.5.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.26" osisID="Matt.5.26" n="26"/>“Truly, I say to you, you will not be able to go out from there until you give even the last eighth.”<verse eID="Matt.5.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.27" osisID="Matt.5.27" n="27"/>“You have heard what was written, ‘You shall not commit adultery,’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="38" osisRef="Matt.5.27" osisID="Matt.5.27!note.38">Literally, “proclamation” or “declaration.”</note><verse eID="Matt.5.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.28" osisID="Matt.5.28" n="28"/>“I, I say to you, everyone who looked at a woman and lusted after her and is caught in adultery in his heart.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="39" osisRef="Matt.5.28" osisID="Matt.5.28!note.39">See Leviticus 19:17. The Torah commands were always commands of the heart.”</note><verse eID="Matt.5.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.29" osisID="Matt.5.29" n="29"/>“If your right eye causes you to fail, remove it and throw it from you, for it is better that you lose one member from you, and not all your body in Gehinnom.”<verse eID="Matt.5.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.30" osisID="Matt.5.30" n="30"/>“And if your right-hand stumbles you, cut and throw it away. For it is good for you to destroy one member of your body, and not all your body is to Gehinnom.”<verse eID="Matt.5.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.31" osisID="Matt.5.31" n="31"/>“It is said, ‘whoever divorces his wife, will give her a Get of divorce.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="41" osisRef="Matt.5.31" osisID="Matt.5.31!note.41">In this verse, the demons are referred to as “he,” and in the following two verses, they are referenced as “she.”</note><verse eID="Matt.5.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.32" osisID="Matt.5.32" n="32"/>“I say to you, everyone who sends away his wife, except for fornication, is the maker for her of adultery, and also whoever marries her, the divorcee, commits adultery.”<verse eID="Matt.5.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.33" osisID="Matt.5.33" n="33"/>“Again, you have heard those of old who said, ‘You shall not support <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="43" osisRef="Matt.5.33" osisID="Matt.5.33!note.43">Can mean to “profess” or “support.”</note>a false oath, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="44" osisRef="Matt.5.33" osisID="Matt.5.33!note.44">Can also translate to “eviler.”</note>but you shall fulfill it to the owner of your oath.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="45" osisRef="Matt.5.33" osisID="Matt.5.33!note.45">This can also be translated as “and so will it be for this evil generation,” as this is speaking of a different group of family members at a</note><verse eID="Matt.5.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.34" osisID="Matt.5.34" n="34"/>“I say, do not swear by the heavens before the throne of Elohim (God),”<verse eID="Matt.5.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.35" osisID="Matt.5.35" n="35"/>“and not by the earth, because it is the footstool of His feet, and neither by Yerushalim (Jerusalem), because it is the land belonging to the King of the world.”<verse eID="Matt.5.35"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.36" osisID="Matt.5.36" n="36"/>“you should not swear by your head, for you are unable to make one hair either black or white.”<verse eID="Matt.5.36"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.37" osisID="Matt.5.37" n="37"/>“However, let your words be concerning, ‘Yes, yes,’ and concerning, ‘No, no;’ and if it passes from this, it is evil.”<verse eID="Matt.5.37"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.38" osisID="Matt.5.38" n="38"/>“You have heard what was said, ‘An eye in exchange for an eye, A tooth in exchange for a tooth.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="48" osisRef="Matt.5.38" osisID="Matt.5.38!note.48">Exodus 21:23–24; Leviticus 24:19–20; Deuteronomy 19:21.</note><verse eID="Matt.5.38"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.39" osisID="Matt.5.39" n="39"/>“I, I say to you, do not rise up to do evil, but whoever strikes upon one’s right cheek, shall also show him the left.”<verse eID="Matt.5.39"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.40" osisID="Matt.5.40" n="40"/>“And whoever wants to sentence you to take your turban, leave it to him, even your undershirt!”<verse eID="Matt.5.40"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.41" osisID="Matt.5.41" n="41"/>“Whoever wants you to walk a parasang, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="51" osisRef="Matt.5.41" osisID="Matt.5.41!note.51">“A parasang is a length or measurement of distance used in what is now Iran. It varied according to the region. The north-eastern parasang was about 15,000 paces; the north-western parasang was 18,000 paces, and the one of the southwest was merely 6,000 paces. The measurement called the “true parasang” was about 9,000 paces.” The Books of Enoch: The Angels, The Watchers and The Nephilim, pp. 225. See 3 Enoch 5: The Hebrew Book of Enoch for the use of the word, “parasang.” For 3 Enoch is believed to have been written around c. 100 CE.</note>walk with him two parasangs.<verse eID="Matt.5.41"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.42" osisID="Matt.5.42" n="42"/>“To whoever that asks a question, you must give to him; or whoever borrows from you, do not withhold it.”<verse eID="Matt.5.42"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.43" osisID="Matt.5.43" n="43"/>“You have heard that they said, ‘For you love your neighbor and hate your haters.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="54" osisRef="Matt.5.43" osisID="Matt.5.43!note.54">See Leviticus 19:18.</note><verse eID="Matt.5.43"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.44" osisID="Matt.5.44" n="44"/>“I say to you, ‘Have mercy on your enemies, and bless whoever curses you, and do good to those cursing you, and pray for those who memorialize to do evils and persecution;’”<verse eID="Matt.5.44"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.45" osisID="Matt.5.45" n="45"/>“and then you will be children of your Father in the heavens, who created the sun over good and over bad, and also brings down rain on good and bad, and the righteous and the wicked.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="55" osisRef="Matt.5.45" osisID="Matt.5.45!note.55">See Exodus 23:4-5 when Elohim (God) commanded His people to be merciful to their enemies.</note><verse eID="Matt.5.45"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.46" osisID="Matt.5.46" n="46"/>“And if you love those who love you, what reward do you have? For you are like the children of the market doing this!<verse eID="Matt.5.46"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.47" osisID="Matt.5.47" n="47"/>“And if they ask in peace of your brothers only, what good is there in you? Even the children of marketplaces do so!”<verse eID="Matt.5.47"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.5.48" osisID="Matt.5.48" n="48"/>“You, accordingly, will be complete, as your Father in heaven who is complete.<verse eID="Matt.5.48"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.5"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.6" osisID="Matt.6" n="6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.1" osisID="Matt.6.1" n="1"/>You shall see when you give you charity, to not give it before man, because if they see, there will not be a reward for you from your Father in the heavens.<verse eID="Matt.6.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.2" osisID="Matt.6.2" n="2"/>When doing righteousness, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="4" osisRef="Matt.6.2" osisID="Matt.6.2!note.4">The pronoun for “he” or “it” is an added pronoun as it is included in the word ענה (ana, “he/it answered).. It is possible the author intended to make it present tense.</note>do not announce in the streets and markets in order to be praised before the people, or you will lose your reward.<verse eID="Matt.6.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.3" osisID="Matt.6.3" n="3"/>However, when you do righteousness, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="5" osisRef="Matt.6.3" osisID="Matt.6.3!note.5">Genesis 1:27.</note>it should be in secret as such: do not let even your left hand know the right hand is doing,<verse eID="Matt.6.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.4" osisID="Matt.6.4" n="4"/>so your righteousness is in secret. <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="6" osisRef="Matt.6.4" osisID="Matt.6.4!note.6">This verse is comparable to Yehovah speaking from Heaven in Matthew 3:17, and in the Cochin Matthew 3:17, it states, “...and to him,</note>Then Elohim (God) who sees in secret shall reward you by openly revealing.<verse eID="Matt.6.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.5" osisID="Matt.6.5" n="5"/>And when you pray, your prayer should not be short-term like those who are accustomed to stand and pray in the markets and streets before the eyes of the people, for you will lose your reward.<verse eID="Matt.6.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.6" osisID="Matt.6.6" n="6"/>However, when you pray, enter your house and close your doors and pray to your hidden Elohim (God). And He who sees in secret, rewards you by openly revealing.<verse eID="Matt.6.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.7" osisID="Matt.6.7" n="7"/>And whoever is praying, do not speak like the hypocrites who speak much in order to be heard.<verse eID="Matt.6.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.8" osisID="Matt.6.8" n="8"/>Don’t imitate them, for your Father who is in heaven answers you before you ask Him.<verse eID="Matt.6.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.9" osisID="Matt.6.9" n="9"/>But, therefore, pray as so to your Father, and His name shall be sanctified.<verse eID="Matt.6.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.10" osisID="Matt.6.10" n="10"/>“May your kingdom be true, certain and approved, beneath the heavens and also on the earth.”<verse eID="Matt.6.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.11" osisID="Matt.6.11" n="11"/>“Give us food of the word <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="10" osisRef="Matt.6.11" osisID="Matt.6.11!note.10">Deuteronomy 24:1.</note>daily in its day.”<verse eID="Matt.6.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.12" osisID="Matt.6.12" n="12"/>“And forgive us of our debts, as we forgive our debtors.”<verse eID="Matt.6.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.13" osisID="Matt.6.13" n="13"/>“Do not bring us to a trial <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="11" osisRef="Matt.6.13" osisID="Matt.6.13!note.11">In Deuteronomy 24:1, the Hebrew word used for “writing” is סֵפֶר (sefer), which means, “book, scroll, writing.” However, in this verse, the author</note>but rescue us from all evil. For it is your kingdom, and your power, and your glory forever and ever.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="12" osisRef="Matt.6.13" osisID="Matt.6.13!note.12">Literal meanings: “drives out, thrusts, expels.”</note><verse eID="Matt.6.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.14" osisID="Matt.6.14" n="14"/>If you forgive others, HaShem (Yehovah) <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="13" osisRef="Matt.6.14" osisID="Matt.6.14!note.13">This is a harsh word that can mean “whore, fornicator,” or “prostitute.” The proper word for “adulterer” is נואף (no’ef).</note>will forgive you of your sins.”<verse eID="Matt.6.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.15" osisID="Matt.6.15" n="15"/>But if you do not forgive sons of man, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="14" osisRef="Matt.6.15" osisID="Matt.6.15!note.14">Collectively, including details from Matthew 5:32 and Luke 16:18, along with this passage, the man who marries the first wife will be an adulterer נואף (no’ef) because the woman was not divorced properly with her כריתות (keritut, “divorcement”). The woman who is separated, or rather “put away” גרושה (gerusha), is still considered married. The man in Matthew 19:9 makes himself a fornicator זונה (zonah) by taking a 2nd woman without</note>HaShem (Yehovah) will not forgive you of your sins.<verse eID="Matt.6.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.16" osisID="Matt.6.16" n="16"/>When you are afflicted, do not see yourselves as hungry, but change your faces in front of people to demonstrate being tortured, and lose your reward.<verse eID="Matt.6.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.17" osisID="Matt.6.17" n="17"/>When you fast, your face and your head are washed,<verse eID="Matt.6.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.18" osisID="Matt.6.18" n="18"/>so that sons of man do not see you are in a fast, but your Father who is hidden and sees in secret, shall repay you.<verse eID="Matt.6.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.19" osisID="Matt.6.19" n="19"/>Do not set aside for yourselves hidden treasures on the earth, in a place of worms consuming and destroying, and as well as thieves sneaking in and stealing.<verse eID="Matt.6.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.20" osisID="Matt.6.20" n="20"/>But place your hidden treasures in the heavens where there are no worms to consume, and a place that thieves do not sneak in and steal.<verse eID="Matt.6.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.21" osisID="Matt.6.21" n="21"/>From the place which your treasures are, there will be your desire.<verse eID="Matt.6.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.22" osisID="Matt.6.22" n="22"/>The lamp of your body is the eye; and if your eye is confident, all your body will be a light.<verse eID="Matt.6.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.23" osisID="Matt.6.23" n="23"/>If your eye is wicked, all your body will be dark; therefore, if this light that is in you is dark, everything will be dark.<verse eID="Matt.6.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.24" osisID="Matt.6.24" n="24"/>No man will be able to serve two masters, for one he will hate and love another, or one he will value one or despise the other one; and you are not able to serve, likewise, Elohim (God) and mammon.<verse eID="Matt.6.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.25" osisID="Matt.6.25" n="25"/>And thus, I tell you to not loathe yourselves, saying, “What shall I eat, and what shall I drink?” nor for your body likewise, “What will you wear:” but is not the soul greater than mammon and your body than clothing?”<verse eID="Matt.6.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.26" osisID="Matt.6.26" n="26"/>And you will see the birds of the heavens: they do not sow, nor harvest, and do not have treasure in the storehouse; but HaShem (Yehovah) provides for them. Are you not abundantly above them?<verse eID="Matt.6.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.27" osisID="Matt.6.27" n="27"/>Who among you that by wearying are able to add onto his height one cubit?<verse eID="Matt.6.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.28" osisID="Matt.6.28" n="28"/>And if you loathe about clothing, you should look at the flowers of the wilderness, how they grow, which is not caused by fatigue.<verse eID="Matt.6.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.29" osisID="Matt.6.29" n="29"/>I tell you, even Shelomoh (Solomon), the king in all his splendor, was not clothed as one of them.<verse eID="Matt.6.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.30" osisID="Matt.6.30" n="30"/>If this grain of the field, that exists today and tomorrow, falls into the furnace, Elohim (God) sufficiently clothes. May not be the same for you, you of little faith?<verse eID="Matt.6.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.31" osisID="Matt.6.31" n="31"/>Do not fret by saying, “What will we eat?” or “What will we drink?” or “What will we be clothed?”<verse eID="Matt.6.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.32" osisID="Matt.6.32" n="32"/>All this the peoples of the lands seek. But your Father, who is in the heavens, knows you that you seek all this.<verse eID="Matt.6.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.33" osisID="Matt.6.33" n="33"/>You should request this from the Lord of the heavens, for it is his righteousness; and therefore, it will be added to you.<verse eID="Matt.6.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.6.34" osisID="Matt.6.34" n="34"/>You should not dread about the tomorrows, because tomorrow will have its own worry, and enough for a person is the evil of the day.”<verse eID="Matt.6.34"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.6"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.7" osisID="Matt.7" n="7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.1" osisID="Matt.7.1" n="1"/>You shall not judge, so you will not be judged.<verse eID="Matt.7.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.2" osisID="Matt.7.2" n="2"/>Just as you judge your friend, it will be judged to you, and with the measurement that you measure: with it, you will be corrected.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="5" osisRef="Matt.7.2" osisID="Matt.7.2!note.5">Genesis 1:27.</note><verse eID="Matt.7.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.3" osisID="Matt.7.3" n="3"/>But you see the chip of wood in the eye of your friend, but the beam that is in your eye, you do not see?<verse eID="Matt.7.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.4" osisID="Matt.7.4" n="4"/>How can you say to your brother, “Take the chip of wood from between your tooth,” but here is a beam between your eye?<verse eID="Matt.7.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.5" osisID="Matt.7.5" n="5"/>You denier! Remove the beam from between your eyes beforehand, and later remove the chip of wood that is between the eyes of your brother.<verse eID="Matt.7.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.6" osisID="Matt.7.6" n="6"/>Do not throw a holy matter <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="10" osisRef="Matt.7.6" osisID="Matt.7.6!note.10">Deuteronomy 24:1.</note>before the dogs, and do not throw pearls before the pigs, for it may be that they trample them with their feet and in turn, injure you.<verse eID="Matt.7.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.7" osisID="Matt.7.7" n="7"/>Ask and I will give to you, seek and you will find it, and be strong <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="14" osisRef="Matt.7.7" osisID="Matt.7.7!note.14">Collectively, including details from Matthew 5:32 and Luke 16:18, along with this passage, the man who marries the first wife will be an adulterer נואף (no’ef) because the woman was not divorced properly with her כריתות (keritut, “divorcement”). The woman who is separated, or rather “put away” גרושה (gerusha), is still considered married. The man in Matthew 19:9 makes himself a fornicator זונה (zonah) by taking a 2nd woman without</note>at the entrance and I will open it for you.<verse eID="Matt.7.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.8" osisID="Matt.7.8" n="8"/>So, everyone who asks, receives, and everyone who seeks, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="15" osisRef="Matt.7.8" osisID="Matt.7.8!note.15">Can also be translated to, “men with a woman.”</note>finds. <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="16" osisRef="Matt.7.8" osisID="Matt.7.8!note.16">In Matthew 16:19, Yeshua is speaking directly to Peter about building an assembly upon the stone of truth that Yeshua is the Messiah by saying, “To you I will give the keys of heaven and all of what you prohibit ( אוסר oser) in the world will be prohibited in the heavens, and all of what remains will be permitted in the heaven.” However, in Matthew 18:18, Yeshua is speaking directly to all the disciples about handling conflict in the community by saying, “...all what is bound ( קשור kishur) on earth, is bound in the heavens: and what is opened on earth will be opened in the heavens.” These are speaking of two different truths: 1. The truth that Yeshua is the Messiah is the foundation of the new covenant assembly. However, if “the heavens” is an idiom of “heavenly beings,” according to Joseph A. Fitzmeyer’s analysis of the 1350 Hittite treaty (See the English Edition of the Aramaic text, with references to biblical parallels, in Joseph A. Fitzmyer, trans., “The Inscriptions of Bar-Ga’yah and Mati’el from Sefire,” The Context of Scripture 2.82 (W. Hallo and K. L. Younger, eds. [Leiden: Brill, 2000]), 213–215), then the keys to the heavens appear to be keys that permit or prohibit heavenly beings in situations such as when an individual is turned over the satan for destruction of the flesh and correction when Paul turned two individuals over to Satan in 1 Corinthians 5:5, and 1 Timothy 1:20. Even though Yeshua was speaking directly to Peter, and Paul, who was not a disciple of Yeshua but was sent by Yeshua, was given LEGAL AUTHORITY over the assemblies was able to turn these men over to Satan. This can also be done by those who have legal authority over the assembly, but it is recommended that you ask Yehovah to fight the fight on their behalf, other than attempting to face off with demonic entities. 2. In Matthew 18:18, the disciples are taught how to resolve conflicts and to be humble with a forgiving heart. This verse seems to indicate that “all what is bound,” meaning either by man or God, is bound in the heavens. Likewise, all that is opened on earth, by man or God, is opened in the heavens. Therefore, if a person “binds” something on earth, such as a spiritual being, then it is bound in the heavens. But if something is “opened” on earth — like a modern television, radio, internet, or a cursed item — then it is opened upon the</note>And everyone who knocks, it is opened.<verse eID="Matt.7.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.9" osisID="Matt.7.9" n="9"/>What kind of man is he from among you, whose son asks him for bread, provides him a stone?<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="17" osisRef="Matt.7.9" osisID="Matt.7.9!note.17">See Isaiah 6:9–10.</note><verse eID="Matt.7.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.10" osisID="Matt.7.10" n="10"/>And if he asks for a fish, would he provide him a snake?<verse eID="Matt.7.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.11" osisID="Matt.7.11" n="11"/>And even if you being evil know how to give good gifts to your children, how much more does HaShem (Yehovah)<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="18" osisRef="Matt.7.11" osisID="Matt.7.11!note.18">The Nit’pael verb binyan is a mix of the Nif’al and Hit’pael verb binyanim and was used regularly in Mishnaic Hebrew to express a</note> give good gifts to His askers?<verse eID="Matt.7.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.12" osisID="Matt.7.12" n="12"/>Just as you desire that people do your will, thus you should do their desire; for the prophets commanded so.<verse eID="Matt.7.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.13" osisID="Matt.7.13" n="13"/>Enter into the narrow gate, to the wide opened door, and in the path is a spacious place for those who are lost go; for many men go there.<verse eID="Matt.7.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.14" osisID="Matt.7.14" n="14"/>How small is the gate and narrow path that leads to life, and few are they that are found there?<verse eID="Matt.7.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.15" osisID="Matt.7.15" n="15"/>Beware of the false prophets <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="19" osisRef="Matt.7.15" osisID="Matt.7.15!note.19">This compelling verse highlights that Yeshua urges us: if we seek eternal life, we must follow His commandments. See Leviticus 18:5, Ezekiel</note>who come near to you in clothes of wool on the outside but inside are full of trickeries of war.<verse eID="Matt.7.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.16" osisID="Matt.7.16" n="16"/>From their fruits you will know them; for how can they gather grapes from thorns, and figs from thistles?<verse eID="Matt.7.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.17" osisID="Matt.7.17" n="17"/>Therefore, there is a good tree and its fruits are good; and a bad <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="20" osisRef="Matt.7.17" osisID="Matt.7.17!note.20">This word is first found in Mishnah Avodah Zara 5:3.</note>tree, its fruits are bad.<verse eID="Matt.7.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.18" osisID="Matt.7.18" n="18"/>A good tree cannot be found having bad fruits, and neither can a bad tree have good fruits.<verse eID="Matt.7.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.19" osisID="Matt.7.19" n="19"/>Every tree that does not produce good fruit, they cut it down and throw into the fire.<verse eID="Matt.7.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.20" osisID="Matt.7.20" n="20"/>And from their fruit, you will know them.<verse eID="Matt.7.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.21" osisID="Matt.7.21" n="21"/>Not everyone <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="23" osisRef="Matt.7.21" osisID="Matt.7.21!note.23">This appears to be a misspelled word and should be תִּלְּמְדו “you will learn.” It appears to be a defective spelling of “you (mp) will learn.”</note>who says to me, “My Master, my Master,” will ascend to the heavens, but it is whoever does the desire of my Father in the heavens.<verse eID="Matt.7.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.22" osisID="Matt.7.22" n="22"/>Many will say on the day, “My Master, my Master — but did we not prophesy in your name, and in your name drive out demons, and in your name do many extraordinary things?”<verse eID="Matt.7.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.23" osisID="Matt.7.23" n="23"/>Then I will acknowledge them with an oath, saying, “I have not known you, but be removed from me, you workers of iniquity!”<verse eID="Matt.7.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.24" osisID="Matt.7.24" n="24"/>Everyone who hears these words from me and does accordingly, he is like a wise man and builds his house upon the hills.<verse eID="Matt.7.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.25" osisID="Matt.7.25" n="25"/>And its rain descended, and rivers came, and wind blew to destroy it (the house). But it did not fall because its foundation was marked upon the hills.<verse eID="Matt.7.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.26" osisID="Matt.7.26" n="26"/>And everyone who hears and does not do them is like a fool who built his house on sand.<verse eID="Matt.7.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.27" osisID="Matt.7.27" n="27"/>And rain descended, and a river came, then the wind blew and caused destruction, and the house fell, and it was a great fall.<verse eID="Matt.7.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.28" osisID="Matt.7.28" n="28"/>When the words of Yeshua were completed, all the people were astonished over the teaching.<verse eID="Matt.7.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.7.29" osisID="Matt.7.29" n="29"/>Because he taught them as an authority, and not like the scribes and sages.<verse eID="Matt.7.29"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.7"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.8" osisID="Matt.8" n="8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.1" osisID="Matt.8.1" n="1"/>When this one descended from the mountain, a vast people surrounded him. The scriptures: And when He came down from the mountain, large crowds followed Him.<verse eID="Matt.8.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.2" osisID="Matt.8.2" n="2"/>Then came one leper who came and bowed down and said, “My Teacher, if you desire, you are able to purify me. The scriptures: And see, a leper came, and bowed before Him, saying, “Master, if You desire, You are able to make me clean.”<verse eID="Matt.8.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.3" osisID="Matt.8.3" n="3"/>And he straightened out his hand and said, “I desire to purify you. Then within the hour, he was purified.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="4" osisRef="Matt.8.3" osisID="Matt.8.3!note.4">The pronoun for “he” or “it” is an added pronoun as it is included in the word ענה (ana, “he/it answered).. It is possible the author intended to make it present tense.</note> immediately his leprosy was cleansed.<verse eID="Matt.8.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.4" osisID="Matt.8.4" n="4"/>And Yeshua said to him, “See that you do not tell any man, but go to the priest and offer your sacrifice according to Moses in the Torah.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="5" osisRef="Matt.8.4" osisID="Matt.8.4!note.5">Genesis 1:27.</note> the priest, and offer the gift that Mosheh commanded, as a witness to them.”<verse eID="Matt.8.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.5" osisID="Matt.8.5" n="5"/>After this, Yeshua went up to Kephar Nahum, and one official approached him and asked him and said,<verse eID="Matt.8.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.6" osisID="Matt.8.6" n="6"/>“My Lord, my boy has fallen in my house and became a madman and is laid down to die.” The scriptures: saying, “Master, my servant is lying in the house paralysed, grievously tortured.”<verse eID="Matt.8.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.7" osisID="Matt.8.7" n="7"/>Yeshua said to him, “I will come to heal him.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="6" osisRef="Matt.8.7" osisID="Matt.8.7!note.6">This verse is comparable to Yehovah speaking from Heaven in Matthew 3:17, and in the Cochin Matthew 3:17, it states, “...and to him,</note><verse eID="Matt.8.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.8" osisID="Matt.8.8" n="8"/>The man replied to him and said, “My teacher, it is not my desire that you come under my rafter, but rather it is enough that you say one word and the boy will recover.” The scriptures: And the captain answering, said, “Master, I am not worthy that You should come under my roof. But only say a word, and my servant shall be healed.<verse eID="Matt.8.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.9" osisID="Matt.8.9" n="9"/>“Moreso, I that have mamona <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="7" osisRef="Matt.8.9" osisID="Matt.8.9!note.7">See Exodus 21:17 and Leviticus 20:9.</note>(money) from the kingdom, and in the shadow of my rafter, there are people. So, if I say to them, ‘You go,’ and they go. And to another I say, ‘Come,’ and he comes. And to my servant I say, ‘Do accordingly,’ as he does.” The scriptures: For I too am a man under authority, having soldiers under me. And I say to this one, ‘Go,’ and he goes, and to another, ‘Come,’ and he comes, and to my servant, ‘Do this,’ and he does it.<verse eID="Matt.8.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.10" osisID="Matt.8.10" n="10"/>When Yeshua heard, he was amazed and said to those with him, “Truth I say to you, that even with the children of Israel, there does not exist faith like this!” you, not even in Yisra’ĕl have I found such great belief! Now, when Eshu {Yeshua} heard this, He was amazed, and said unto those that were coming with Him, “Amiyn {Truly} I say unto you, that not even in IysraIyl {Israel} have I found Haymanutha {Faith} like this!<verse eID="Matt.8.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.11" osisID="Matt.8.11" n="11"/>“Yet, I say that many have come from the east and from the west to lean upon the table with Avraham (Abraham), and Yitzhaq (Jacob) in the kingdom of heaven.” The scriptures: “And I say to you that many shall come from east and west, and sit down with Abṟaham, and Yitsḥaq, and Ya‛aqoḇ in the reign of the heavens,<verse eID="Matt.8.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.12" osisID="Matt.8.12" n="12"/>Children of this kingdom will be expelled into darkness outside, and there they will be weeping and gnashing<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="9" osisRef="Matt.8.12" osisID="Matt.8.12!note.9">Or may also mean, “against you.” It also appears that the scribe may not have intended to write the word אני (ani, “I”), with the middle letter being the letter nun, but instead, he may have intended to write the word אוי (oy, “Woe!), with the middle letter being a vav. Thus, if</note> their teeth. The scriptures: but the sons of the reign shall be cast out into outer darkness – there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth.”<verse eID="Matt.8.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.13" osisID="Matt.8.13" n="13"/>And Yeshua said to the man, “You go; just as you believe, it will be for you.” And in that hour, the boy was healed. And his servant was healed that hour.<verse eID="Matt.8.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.14" osisID="Matt.8.14" n="14"/>And afterwards, Yeshua came to the house of Shim’on (Simeon), who is called Pedro (Peter). And he saw his mother-in-law hurled and seized with a fever. sick with inflammation.<verse eID="Matt.8.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.15" osisID="Matt.8.15" n="15"/>And he grasped her hand, and the fever went from her. Then she rose and served them. The scriptures: And He touched her hand, and the inflammation left her. And she arose and served them.<verse eID="Matt.8.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.16" osisID="Matt.8.16" n="16"/>When it was night, they drew near to him people who were epileptic by demons and expelled them by a whisper. And all of those who had something bad, he also healed, The scriptures: And when evening had come, they brought to Him many who were demon-possessed. And He cast out the spirits with a word, and healed all who were sick,<verse eID="Matt.8.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.17" osisID="Matt.8.17" n="17"/>so as to fulfill the saying of the prophet Yeshayah (Isaiah): “And he borne our pains and our sicknesses.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="16" osisRef="Matt.8.17" osisID="Matt.8.17!note.16">In Matthew 16:19, Yeshua is speaking directly to Peter about building an assembly upon the stone of truth that Yeshua is the Messiah by saying, “To you I will give the keys of heaven and all of what you prohibit ( אוסר oser) in the world will be prohibited in the heavens, and all of what remains will be permitted in the heaven.” However, in Matthew 18:18, Yeshua is speaking directly to all the disciples about handling conflict in the community by saying, “...all what is bound ( קשור kishur) on earth, is bound in the heavens: and what is opened on earth will be opened in the heavens.” These are speaking of two different truths: 1. The truth that Yeshua is the Messiah is the foundation of the new covenant assembly. However, if “the heavens” is an idiom of “heavenly beings,” according to Joseph A. Fitzmeyer’s analysis of the 1350 Hittite treaty (See the English Edition of the Aramaic text, with references to biblical parallels, in Joseph A. Fitzmyer, trans., “The Inscriptions of Bar-Ga’yah and Mati’el from Sefire,” The Context of Scripture 2.82 (W. Hallo and K. L. Younger, eds. [Leiden: Brill, 2000]), 213–215), then the keys to the heavens appear to be keys that permit or prohibit heavenly beings in situations such as when an individual is turned over the satan for destruction of the flesh and correction when Paul turned two individuals over to Satan in 1 Corinthians 5:5, and 1 Timothy 1:20. Even though Yeshua was speaking directly to Peter, and Paul, who was not a disciple of Yeshua but was sent by Yeshua, was given LEGAL AUTHORITY over the assemblies was able to turn these men over to Satan. This can also be done by those who have legal authority over the assembly, but it is recommended that you ask Yehovah to fight the fight on their behalf, other than attempting to face off with demonic entities. 2. In Matthew 18:18, the disciples are taught how to resolve conflicts and to be humble with a forgiving heart. This verse seems to indicate that “all what is bound,” meaning either by man or God, is bound in the heavens. Likewise, all that is opened on earth, by man or God, is opened in the heavens. Therefore, if a person “binds” something on earth, such as a spiritual being, then it is bound in the heavens. But if something is “opened” on earth — like a modern television, radio, internet, or a cursed item — then it is opened upon the</note> The scriptures: that it might be filled what was spoken by Yeshayahu the prophet, saying, “He Himself took our weaknesses and bore our sicknesses.”<verse eID="Matt.8.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.18" osisID="Matt.8.18" n="18"/>When Yeshua saw that many people gathered together around him, he commanded them to go to the other side. other side.<verse eID="Matt.8.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.19" osisID="Matt.8.19" n="19"/>Then one sage beside him and said to him, “Rabbi, wherever you go, I will follow you.” The scriptures: And a certain scribe, having come near, said to Him, “Teacher, I shall follow You wherever You go.”<verse eID="Matt.8.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.20" osisID="Matt.8.20" n="20"/>Yeshua said to him, “The foxes have holes, and the birds of the heavens have nests, but this Son of man has no place that he can rest his head.” Son of Ada̱m has nowhere to lay His head.”<verse eID="Matt.8.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.21" osisID="Matt.8.21" n="21"/>One of his talmidim (students) said to him, “My teacher, give me permission to go bury my father first.” The scriptures: And another of His taught ones said to Him, “Master, first let me go and bury my father.”<verse eID="Matt.8.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.22" osisID="Matt.8.22" n="22"/>In that hour, Yeshua said to him, “You are to come follow me and leave the dead, and the dead will bury him.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="18" osisRef="Matt.8.22" osisID="Matt.8.22!note.18">The Nit’pael verb binyan is a mix of the Nif’al and Hit’pael verb binyanim and was used regularly in Mishnaic Hebrew to express a</note><verse eID="Matt.8.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.23" osisID="Matt.8.23" n="23"/>And when Yeshua entered in the ship, his talmidim (students) were with him. The scriptures: And when He entered into a boat, His taught ones followed Him.<verse eID="Matt.8.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.24" osisID="Matt.8.24" n="24"/>And behold, the wind of a storm was in the sea until the ship was going to sink from the waves of the sea ‒ but Yeshua was asleep.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="19" osisRef="Matt.8.24" osisID="Matt.8.24!note.19">This compelling verse highlights that Yeshua urges us: if we seek eternal life, we must follow His commandments. See Leviticus 18:5, Ezekiel</note> The scriptures: And see, a great gale arose on the sea, so that the boat was covered by the waves. But He was sleeping.<verse eID="Matt.8.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.25" osisID="Matt.8.25" n="25"/>And his talmidim (students) woke him and said, “Our Lord, behold, we are perishing!” The scriptures: And His taught ones came to Him and woke Him up, saying, “Master, save us! We are perishing!” and His Disciples approached Him, woke Him, and said unto Him, “Maran {Our Lord}, deliver us, we are perishing!”<verse eID="Matt.8.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.26" osisID="Matt.8.26" n="26"/>Yeshua said to them, “Why are you afraid, who are of little faith?” Then he rose and rebuked the wind and the sea, and it calmed from its tempest. The scriptures: And He said to them, “Why are you afraid, O you of little belief?” Then, having risen, He rebuked the winds and the sea. And there was a great calm.<verse eID="Matt.8.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.27" osisID="Matt.8.27" n="27"/>These people were astonished and said, “Who is he that the wind and the sea listen to him?” The scriptures: And the men marvelled, saying, “What is this, that even the winds and the sea obey Him?”<verse eID="Matt.8.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.28" osisID="Matt.8.28" n="28"/>And after Yeshua came to cross over to that side, two sorcerers met him, who went out from among the tombs, so much evil that it was not possible for any people to pass that same path. The scriptures: And when He came to the other side, to the country of the Girgashites, two demon- possessed ones met Him, coming out of the tombs, very fierce, so that no one was able to pass that way.<verse eID="Matt.8.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.29" osisID="Matt.8.29" n="29"/>And they shouted and said, “What do we have to do with you, Yeshua, Son of Elohim (God)? You came here to irritate us before the appointed time?” Have You come here to torture us, before the appointed time?”<verse eID="Matt.8.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.30" osisID="Matt.8.30" n="30"/>And at that remote place from them, there was a stable of many pigs and also herdsmen. The scriptures: And at a distance from them there was a herd of many pigs feeding,<verse eID="Matt.8.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.31" osisID="Matt.8.31" n="31"/>And those demons begged him and said, “If you expel us from this place, we will go in the nearby pigs.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="21" osisRef="Matt.8.31" osisID="Matt.8.31!note.21">Literally, “he/it lent.” It appears the author intended to write it as a noun, but wrote as a verb.</note> The scriptures: and the demons begged Him, saying, “If You cast us out, send us into the herd of pigs.”<verse eID="Matt.8.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.32" osisID="Matt.8.32" n="32"/>And Yeshua said to them, “You, go!” And after they left, they went in the nearby pigs to the stable, and the herdsmen ran to encompass them, but they fell in the sea and died in the water. The scriptures: And He said to them, “Go.” And they, coming out, went into the herd of pigs. And see, the entire herd of pigs rushed down the steep place into the sea, and died in the water.<verse eID="Matt.8.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.33" osisID="Matt.8.33" n="33"/>Those of the herdsmen that escaped and left for the province then told all that occurred and the matters of the adversaries (satans). The scriptures: And the herdsmen fled, and went away into the city and reported all this, and about those possessed by demons.<verse eID="Matt.8.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.8.34" osisID="Matt.8.34" n="34"/>And all the children of the city went out to Yeshua, and when they saw him, they asked of him that he would turn from their area. Him to leave their borders.<verse eID="Matt.8.34"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.8"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.9" osisID="Matt.9" n="9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.1" osisID="Matt.9.1" n="1"/>And he entered into the ship and passed over to its side, and he came to a province, and they brought to him one madman lying on a bed. The scriptures: And entering into a boat, He passed over, and came to His own city.<verse eID="Matt.9.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.2" osisID="Matt.9.2" n="2"/>And Yeshua saw their faith, which they believed in him and said to him with gout, “It is healed, my son, and he will forgive your sins.” belief, said to the paralytic, “Take courage, son, your sins have been forgiven.”<verse eID="Matt.9.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.3" osisID="Matt.9.3" n="3"/>Some people, they were thinking in their heart, “He blasphemes!” The scriptures: And see, some of the scribes said to themselves, “This One blasphemes!”<verse eID="Matt.9.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.4" osisID="Matt.9.4" n="4"/>Yeshua knew their thoughts and said to them, “Why are you thinking evil in your heart?”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="7" osisRef="Matt.9.4" osisID="Matt.9.4!note.7">See Exodus 21:17 and Leviticus 20:9.</note> hearts?<verse eID="Matt.9.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.5" osisID="Matt.9.5" n="5"/>Which is more appropriate to say, “’Your sins have been forgiven you?’ or to say, ‘Rise, go’”? The scriptures: “For which is easier, to say, ‘Your sins have been forgiven,’ or to say, ‘Arise and walk’?<verse eID="Matt.9.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.6" osisID="Matt.9.6" n="6"/>And you will know that the Son of man has power on the earth to forgiven sins. Then he said to him who was sick, “Rise, take your bed and go to your house!” The scriptures: “But in order for you to know that the Son of Ada̱m possesses authority on earth to forgive sins...” He then said to the paralytic, “Rise, take up your bed, and go to your house.”<verse eID="Matt.9.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.7" osisID="Matt.9.7" n="7"/>Then he rose and ran to his house. The scriptures: And he rose and went to his house.<verse eID="Matt.9.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.8" osisID="Matt.9.8" n="8"/>When the community saw this, they were frightened and praised El (God) who gave such power to the son of man. The scriptures: And when the crowds saw it, they marvelled and praised Elohim who had given such authority to men.<verse eID="Matt.9.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.9" osisID="Matt.9.9" n="9"/>And when Yeshua went from there, he saw one man in the tax house, and his name was Mattai (Matthew); he said to him, “Follow me.” Then he rose and followed him. office. And He said to him, “Follow Me.” And he rose and followed Him.<verse eID="Matt.9.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.10" osisID="Matt.9.10" n="10"/>And when Yeshua was sitting at the table with his talmidim (students) in his house, people from the tax house came, and many sinners with them. Then they reclined at the table with Yeshua and his talmidim (students). and sinners came and sat down with Him and His taught ones.<verse eID="Matt.9.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.11" osisID="Matt.9.11" n="11"/>When the Pharisees saw, they said to the talmidim (students), “Why with evil people and sinners, does your rabbi eat?” The scriptures: And when the Pharisees saw it, they said to His taught ones, “Why does your Teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?”<verse eID="Matt.9.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.12" osisID="Matt.9.12" n="12"/>When Yeshua heard this, he said to them, “The healthy do not need healing, but those who are sick.” physician, but those who are sick.<verse eID="Matt.9.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.13" osisID="Matt.9.13" n="13"/>Go and learn from me that, “I desire their goodwill and not sacrifice and offering.” <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="9" osisRef="Matt.9.13" osisID="Matt.9.13!note.9">Or may also mean, “against you.” It also appears that the scribe may not have intended to write the word אני (ani, “I”), with the middle letter being the letter nun, but instead, he may have intended to write the word אוי (oy, “Woe!), with the middle letter being a vav. Thus, if</note>I did not come to call the righteous, but sinners.” The scriptures: But go and learn what this means, ‘I desire compassion and not offering.’ For I did not come to call the righteous to repentance, but sinners.<verse eID="Matt.9.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.14" osisID="Matt.9.14" n="14"/>Afterwards, the talmidim (students) of Yochanan (John) drew near to him and said, “Why do we and the Pharisees fast, but your talmidim (students) do not fast?” The scriptures: Then the taught ones of Yoḥanan came to Him, saying, “Why do we and the Pharisees fast often, but Your taught ones do not fast?”<verse eID="Matt.9.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.15" osisID="Matt.9.15" n="15"/>Yeshua said to them, “There are none of the sons of man in happiness with the bridegroom that are fasting, for the bridegroom is with them. However, when the bridegroom goes, then they are fasting.” bridegroom is with them? But the days shall come when the bridegroom is taken away from them, and then they shall fast.<verse eID="Matt.9.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.16" osisID="Matt.9.16" n="16"/>There is no man who puts a new patch on old clothing, because the new patch is torn loose from the old garment. The scriptures: “And no one puts a piece of unshrunk cloth on an old garment, for the patch pulls away from the garment, and the tear is made worse.<verse eID="Matt.9.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.17" osisID="Matt.9.17" n="17"/>And no man puts wine into old skins, so that the skin does not tear, and the wine pours out. But they put new wine into a new skin, and by this the two of them are preserved.” The scriptures: “Neither do they put new wine into old wineskins, or else the wineskins burst, and the wine is spilled, and the wineskins are ruined. But they put new wine into fresh wineskins, and both are preserved.”<verse eID="Matt.9.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.18" osisID="Matt.9.18" n="18"/>And he was still speaking with them, and behold, an important man came and bowed down to him, and said, “Behold, my daughter is declining to death. Come to place your hand upon her so she will live.” The scriptures: While He was saying all this to them, see, a ruler came and bowed down to Him, saying, “My daughter is dead by now, but come and lay Your hand on her and she shall live.”<verse eID="Matt.9.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.19" osisID="Matt.9.19" n="19"/>Then Yeshua rose, and his talmidim (students) left following him.<verse eID="Matt.9.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.20" osisID="Matt.9.20" n="20"/>And behold, a woman who had blood going out of her for twelve years. Then, when he came, she came from behind him to touch his garment. The scriptures: And see, a woman who had a flow of blood for twelve years came from behind and touched the tzitzit of His garment.<verse eID="Matt.9.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.21" osisID="Matt.9.21" n="21"/>She,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="15" osisRef="Matt.9.21" osisID="Matt.9.21!note.15">Can also be translated to, “men with a woman.”</note> she thought in her heart, “If I only take hold of his garment, I will be healed.” The scriptures: For she said to herself, “If I only touch His garment, I shall be healed.”<verse eID="Matt.9.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.22" osisID="Matt.9.22" n="22"/>This Yeshua turned, and saw, and said to her, “My daughter, may you be fulfilled <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="18" osisRef="Matt.9.22" osisID="Matt.9.22!note.18">The Nit’pael verb binyan is a mix of the Nif’al and Hit’pael verb binyanim and was used regularly in Mishnaic Hebrew to express a</note>for your emunah (faith) has brought you down here.” And the woman was healed in that hour. has healed you.” And the woman was healed from that hour.<verse eID="Matt.9.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.23" osisID="Matt.9.23" n="23"/>And Yeshua came to the house of that important man and saw them wailing with a sect of lamenters. making a noise,<verse eID="Matt.9.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.24" osisID="Matt.9.24" n="24"/>Then he said to them, “Get away from the virgin, for she has not died, but she is sleeping.” And they laughed at him. The scriptures: He said to them, “Go back, for the girl is not dead, but sleeping.” And they laughed at Him.<verse eID="Matt.9.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.25" osisID="Matt.9.25" n="25"/>And when he entered with them, he grabbed her by her hands and the daughter rose up. The scriptures: But when the crowd was put outside, He went in and took her by the hand, and the girl arose.<verse eID="Matt.9.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.26" osisID="Matt.9.26" n="26"/>And the rumor went all through the land. The scriptures: And this report went out into all that land.<verse eID="Matt.9.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.27" osisID="Matt.9.27" n="27"/>And when Yeshua came from there, two blind men chased after him, shouting and saying, “Have mercy, Son of David!” saying, “Son of Dawid,̱ have compassion on us!”<verse eID="Matt.9.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.28" osisID="Matt.9.28" n="28"/>And when Yeshua came from there to a house, those blind men approached him. He said to them, “Do you have faith that I am able to do this?” They said, “Yes, my Lord!” “Do you believe that I am able to do this?” They said to Him, “Yes, Master.”<verse eID="Matt.9.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.29" osisID="Matt.9.29" n="29"/>In that hour, he touched their eye and said, “According to your faith, so it will be to you.” The scriptures: Then He touched their eyes, saying, “According to your belief let it be to you.”<verse eID="Matt.9.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.30" osisID="Matt.9.30" n="30"/>And in that hour their eyes were opened, and so Yeshua warned them, “Do not report it to any man.” know.”<verse eID="Matt.9.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.31" osisID="Matt.9.31" n="31"/>After this, they went and announced his goodness all throughout the land. The scriptures: But when they went out, they made Him known in all that land.<verse eID="Matt.9.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.32" osisID="Matt.9.32" n="32"/>When Yeshua went out from there, they brought him to one mute who had a demon. The scriptures: And as they were going out, see, they brought to Him a man, dumb and demon- possessed.<verse eID="Matt.9.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.33" osisID="Matt.9.33" n="33"/>And when he expelled the demon from him, that mute spoke. And then all the people believed and said, “Never has there been found such this in Israel!” The scriptures: And when the demon was cast out, the dumb one spoke. And the crowds marvelled, saying, “It was never seen like this in Yisra’ĕl!”<verse eID="Matt.9.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.34" osisID="Matt.9.34" n="34"/>And the Pharisees said, “Whoever is first taken hold by a demon, expels the demon.” The scriptures: But the Pharisees said, “He casts out demons by the ruler of the demons.”<verse eID="Matt.9.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.35" osisID="Matt.9.35" n="35"/>And Yeshua was returning through all the provinces and villages and teaching in all assemblies, and declaring by the designs of heaven, and healing all sicknesses and disease. proclaiming the Good News of the reign, and healing every disease and every bodily weakness among the people.<verse eID="Matt.9.35"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.36" osisID="Matt.9.36" n="36"/>When he thus saw all the people of the land, he had mercy on them, lest they become worn out sitting like sheep without a shepherd. The scriptures: And having seen the crowds, He was moved with compassion for them, because they were weary and scattered, as sheep having no shepherd.<verse eID="Matt.9.36"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.37" osisID="Matt.9.37" n="37"/>And he said to his talmidim (students), “The harvest is great, but the workers are lesser.” The scriptures: Then He said to His taught ones, “The harvest truly is great, but the workers are few.<verse eID="Matt.9.37"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.9.38" osisID="Matt.9.38" n="38"/>“You are to pray of the owner of the harvest that he would bring out workers for his harvest.” The scriptures: Pray then that the Master of the harvest would send out workers to His harvest.”<verse eID="Matt.9.38"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.9"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.10" osisID="Matt.10" n="10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.1" osisID="Matt.10.1" n="1"/>And he called for his twelve talmidim (students) and vested them with power over all evil spirits, that they would be able to expel them, and taught them to heal all the sick and diseased.<verse eID="Matt.10.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.2" osisID="Matt.10.2" n="2"/>These are the names of his twelve talmidim (students) that came: the first one, Shimon (Simon) called Kepha (Peter), and Andre his brother, and Ya’akov (Jacob) son of (zebdi) Zebedee, and Yochanon (John) his brother,<verse eID="Matt.10.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.3" osisID="Matt.10.3" n="3"/>and Philippos (Philip), and Bartolmiyyahu (Bartholomew), and Toma (Thomas), Mateus (Matthew) the one who preaches<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="4" osisRef="Matt.10.3" osisID="Matt.10.3!note.4">The pronoun for “he” or “it” is an added pronoun as it is included in the word ענה (ana, “he/it answered).. It is possible the author intended to make it present tense.</note> and Ya’akov, the son of Halphai (Alphæus), and Lebohiyyahu (Lebbæus) who is called Taddodiyyahu (Thaddæus),<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="5" osisRef="Matt.10.3" osisID="Matt.10.3!note.5">Genesis 1:27.</note><verse eID="Matt.10.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.4" osisID="Matt.10.4" n="4"/>and Shim’on (Simon) the Canaanite, Yehudah (Judah), Iscariota (Iscariot), <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="6" osisRef="Matt.10.4" osisID="Matt.10.4!note.6">This verse is comparable to Yehovah speaking from Heaven in Matthew 3:17, and in the Cochin Matthew 3:17, it states, “...and to him,</note>who condemned him.<verse eID="Matt.10.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.5" osisID="Matt.10.5" n="5"/>And to these twelve, Yeshua said and commanded them, “Do not walk by the way of pagans, and do not go by the region of the Samaritans,”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="7" osisRef="Matt.10.5" osisID="Matt.10.5!note.7">See Exodus 21:17 and Leviticus 20:9.</note><verse eID="Matt.10.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.6" osisID="Matt.10.6" n="6"/>“go you first to the lost sheep <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="8" osisRef="Matt.10.6" osisID="Matt.10.6!note.8">This word for “divorce” ( יגרש - yegaresh) describes one who “drives out, thrusts out, or expels” his wife.</note>from the house of Yisrael (Israel).”<verse eID="Matt.10.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.7" osisID="Matt.10.7" n="7"/>“And in the hour that you go, you announce and say, ‘The time of the kingdom of heaven has arrived!’”<verse eID="Matt.10.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.8" osisID="Matt.10.8" n="8"/>“Heal <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="10" osisRef="Matt.10.8" osisID="Matt.10.8!note.10">Deuteronomy 24:1.</note>the sick, cleanse the lepers, raise the dead, expel the demons, and whoever redeems kindness to you, reward him with kindness.”<verse eID="Matt.10.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.9" osisID="Matt.10.9" n="9"/>“Do not buy gold, silver, nor copper to put in your pocket,”<verse eID="Matt.10.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.10" osisID="Matt.10.10" n="10"/>“nor shall you make a pocket for bread for the road, nor take two shirts, nor sandals, nor shirts, nor sandals, nor a staff, that they will not say of you, 'It is your false intentions.’”<verse eID="Matt.10.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.11" osisID="Matt.10.11" n="11"/>“In whichever district or village that you enter, ask who is faithful there, and dwell there until you go out from there.”<verse eID="Matt.10.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.12" osisID="Matt.10.12" n="12"/>“And when you who enter, ask for the peace of the men <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="12" osisRef="Matt.10.12" osisID="Matt.10.12!note.12">Literal meanings: “drives out, thrusts, expels.”</note>of the house.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="13" osisRef="Matt.10.12" osisID="Matt.10.12!note.13">This is a harsh word that can mean “whore, fornicator,” or “prostitute.” The proper word for “adulterer” is נואף (no’ef).</note><verse eID="Matt.10.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.13" osisID="Matt.10.13" n="13"/>“And if the head <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="14" osisRef="Matt.10.13" osisID="Matt.10.13!note.14">Collectively, including details from Matthew 5:32 and Luke 16:18, along with this passage, the man who marries the first wife will be an adulterer נואף (no’ef) because the woman was not divorced properly with her כריתות (keritut, “divorcement”). The woman who is separated, or rather “put away” גרושה (gerusha), is still considered married. The man in Matthew 19:9 makes himself a fornicator זונה (zonah) by taking a 2nd woman without</note>of the house is one who initially seeks your peace; your peace will be lost to you.”<verse eID="Matt.10.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.14" osisID="Matt.10.14" n="14"/>“And if they do not receive or listen to your speaking, when you go out of the house or the city, you are to shake the dust from your foot.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="15" osisRef="Matt.10.14" osisID="Matt.10.14!note.15">Can also be translated to, “men with a woman.”</note><verse eID="Matt.10.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.15" osisID="Matt.10.15" n="15"/>“In truth, I tell you, like Sodom and Amorah (Gomorrah) <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="16" osisRef="Matt.10.15" osisID="Matt.10.15!note.16">In Matthew 16:19, Yeshua is speaking directly to Peter about building an assembly upon the stone of truth that Yeshua is the Messiah by saying, “To you I will give the keys of heaven and all of what you prohibit ( אוסר oser) in the world will be prohibited in the heavens, and all of what remains will be permitted in the heaven.” However, in Matthew 18:18, Yeshua is speaking directly to all the disciples about handling conflict in the community by saying, “...all what is bound ( קשור kishur) on earth, is bound in the heavens: and what is opened on earth will be opened in the heavens.” These are speaking of two different truths: 1. The truth that Yeshua is the Messiah is the foundation of the new covenant assembly. However, if “the heavens” is an idiom of “heavenly beings,” according to Joseph A. Fitzmeyer’s analysis of the 1350 Hittite treaty (See the English Edition of the Aramaic text, with references to biblical parallels, in Joseph A. Fitzmyer, trans., “The Inscriptions of Bar-Ga’yah and Mati’el from Sefire,” The Context of Scripture 2.82 (W. Hallo and K. L. Younger, eds. [Leiden: Brill, 2000]), 213–215), then the keys to the heavens appear to be keys that permit or prohibit heavenly beings in situations such as when an individual is turned over the satan for destruction of the flesh and correction when Paul turned two individuals over to Satan in 1 Corinthians 5:5, and 1 Timothy 1:20. Even though Yeshua was speaking directly to Peter, and Paul, who was not a disciple of Yeshua but was sent by Yeshua, was given LEGAL AUTHORITY over the assemblies was able to turn these men over to Satan. This can also be done by those who have legal authority over the assembly, but it is recommended that you ask Yehovah to fight the fight on their behalf, other than attempting to face off with demonic entities. 2. In Matthew 18:18, the disciples are taught how to resolve conflicts and to be humble with a forgiving heart. This verse seems to indicate that “all what is bound,” meaning either by man or God, is bound in the heavens. Likewise, all that is opened on earth, by man or God, is opened in the heavens. Therefore, if a person “binds” something on earth, such as a spiritual being, then it is bound in the heavens. But if something is “opened” on earth — like a modern television, radio, internet, or a cursed item — then it is opened upon the</note>it will be appointed for them on the day of judgment for those sons of that province.”<verse eID="Matt.10.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.16" osisID="Matt.10.16" n="16"/>“Behold, I send you as a lamb among wolves; therefore, you will be wise as creatures and innocent as sons of a dove.”<verse eID="Matt.10.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.17" osisID="Matt.10.17" n="17"/>“Beware of the sons of man, who are seen as loving, that they remove you from Elohim (God) to their house of judgment (Beit Din).”<verse eID="Matt.10.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.18" osisID="Matt.10.18" n="18"/>“And deliver before the prince and king, driving you for my sake, to establish their<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="18" osisRef="Matt.10.18" osisID="Matt.10.18!note.18">The Nit’pael verb binyan is a mix of the Nif’al and Hit’pael verb binyanim and was used regularly in Mishnaic Hebrew to express a</note> testimony and the testimony of the people.”<verse eID="Matt.10.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.19" osisID="Matt.10.19" n="19"/>“In the hour that they grievously chastise you, you are not to think about what you will respond to or what will contend against them, for in that hour, you will find what you are to speak,”<verse eID="Matt.10.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.20" osisID="Matt.10.20" n="20"/>“that it is not you who is speaking, but the Spirit of your Father speaking in you.”<verse eID="Matt.10.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.21" osisID="Matt.10.21" n="21"/>“A brother will send his brother over to the judge for death, and a father his son, and the children will rise against their parents and cause them to die.”<verse eID="Matt.10.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.22" osisID="Matt.10.22" n="22"/>“And you will be despised by all the sons of man because of My name; yet, he of mankind, who waits until the end of the matter, he will live.”<verse eID="Matt.10.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.23" osisID="Matt.10.23" n="23"/>“But those that pursue you in one province, you escape in another province; I will say saying to you that ‘You will not complete all the provinces until the Son of man comes!’”<verse eID="Matt.10.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.24" osisID="Matt.10.24" n="24"/>“There is no talmid (student) more informed than his Rabbi, and no servant than his lord.”<verse eID="Matt.10.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.25" osisID="Matt.10.25" n="25"/>“It’s enough for the talmidim (students) to be like his Rabbi, and a servant like his lord; so, if they called the head of the house Ba’al Zebub (Beelzebub), how much more to the children of the house?”<verse eID="Matt.10.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.26" osisID="Matt.10.26" n="26"/>“Therefore, don’t fear them for anything <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="22" osisRef="Matt.10.26" osisID="Matt.10.26!note.22">Literally, “And he saw, his talmidim…”</note>concealed; there is not a thing concealed that will not be revealed, nor hidden that will not be made known.”<verse eID="Matt.10.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.27" osisID="Matt.10.27" n="27"/>“And what I say to you in darkness, you are to say openly. And what your ears hear, you are to proclaim on the rooftops!”<verse eID="Matt.10.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.28" osisID="Matt.10.28" n="28"/>“And you will not fear those who kill the body but are not able to kill the soul. <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="23" osisRef="Matt.10.28" osisID="Matt.10.28!note.23">This appears to be a misspelled word and should be תִּלְּמְדו “you will learn.” It appears to be a defective spelling of “you (mp) will learn.”</note>But be afraid of who is able to kill the body and soul in Gehinnom.”<verse eID="Matt.10.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.29" osisID="Matt.10.29" n="29"/>“Are not two birds sold for one small coin? And not one of them falls to the earth except by the decree of the heavens,”<verse eID="Matt.10.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.30" osisID="Matt.10.30" n="30"/>“for even the hair on your head is numbered one by one.”<verse eID="Matt.10.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.31" osisID="Matt.10.31" n="31"/>“You shall not be very fearful, because you are considered greater than the birds.”<verse eID="Matt.10.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.32" osisID="Matt.10.32" n="32"/>“Every man who acknowledges me before the sons of man, I will acknowledge him before my Father, who is in the heavens.”<verse eID="Matt.10.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.33" osisID="Matt.10.33" n="33"/>“All who deny me before the sons of man, even I, I will deny him before my Father, who is in the heavens.”<verse eID="Matt.10.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.34" osisID="Matt.10.34" n="34"/>“Do not think that I came to allow peace on earth; I have not come except for a sword,”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="24" osisRef="Matt.10.34" osisID="Matt.10.34!note.24">Literally, “If it is you, command that I, he will come…” Thus,</note><verse eID="Matt.10.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.35" osisID="Matt.10.35" n="35"/>“I came but to leave division between a father and his son, between a daughter and her mother, and between a daughter-in-law and her mother-in-law,”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="26" osisRef="Matt.10.35" osisID="Matt.10.35!note.26">Aramaic word that is a metaphor for making it rain. See Aph Dem 4.65:25: ܐܪܛܡ ܬܚܢܘ.</note><verse eID="Matt.10.35"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.36" osisID="Matt.10.36" n="36"/>“and the enemies of a man are men of his house.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="27" osisRef="Matt.10.36" osisID="Matt.10.36!note.27">This word is first used in the Mishnah Berakhot 2:5, “to refrain.”</note><verse eID="Matt.10.36"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.37" osisID="Matt.10.37" n="37"/>“All who want his father or his mother more than me, he is not of me. And all who love his son or daughter more than me, he is not of me.”<verse eID="Matt.10.37"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.38" osisID="Matt.10.38" n="38"/>“And all who do not take up his cross and follow me, he is not of me.”<verse eID="Matt.10.38"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.39" osisID="Matt.10.39" n="39"/>“Whoever loves his soul will lose it. And whoever destroys his soul because of me, I will find him.”<verse eID="Matt.10.39"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.40" osisID="Matt.10.40" n="40"/>“Whoever receives you, receives me. And whoever receives me, receives Him who sent me.”<verse eID="Matt.10.40"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.41" osisID="Matt.10.41" n="41"/>“Whoever receives a prophet in the name of a prophet, takes a reward of a prophet. And whoever receives the righteous in the name of a righteous, takes a reward of the righteous.”<verse eID="Matt.10.41"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.10.42" osisID="Matt.10.42" n="42"/>“And all who give drink to one of the little ones, a glass of cold water only, in the name of the talmidim (students). Truth, I say to you that he will not lose his reward.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="31" osisRef="Matt.10.42" osisID="Matt.10.42!note.31">This word appears to be misspelled, and the vav is in the wrong position and should be תֹּולִים (tolim).</note><verse eID="Matt.10.42"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.10"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.11" osisID="Matt.11" n="11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.1" osisID="Matt.11.1" n="1"/>And it came to be after Yeshua completed commanding his twelve talmidim (students), He changed his location from there to teach and proclaim in their provinces. from there to teach and to proclaim in their cities.<verse eID="Matt.11.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.2" osisID="Matt.11.2" n="2"/>When Yochanan (John) heard in the house of prisoners about the works of the Messiah, he sent by the hand of his talmidim (students) and said to him, The scriptures: And when Yoḥanan had heard in the prison of the works of Messiah, he sent two of his taught ones,<verse eID="Matt.11.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.3" osisID="Matt.11.3" n="3"/>“You are he, whom we are expecting, or do we wait for another and expect him?” The scriptures: and said to Him, “Are You the Coming One, or do we look for another?”<verse eID="Matt.11.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.4" osisID="Matt.11.4" n="4"/>Yeshua said, “Go to Yochanan (John) and say, “Such as that you heard, you will see.”<verse eID="Matt.11.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.5" osisID="Matt.11.5" n="5"/>“The blind see, and the lame walk, the leper is healed, the dead rise, and the poor are enriched.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="4" osisRef="Matt.11.5" osisID="Matt.11.5!note.4">The pronoun for “he” or “it” is an added pronoun as it is included in the word ענה (ana, “he/it answered).. It is possible the author intended to make it present tense.</note> The scriptures: “Blind receive sight and lame walk, lepers are cleansed and deaf hear, dead are raised up and poor are brought the Good News.<verse eID="Matt.11.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.6" osisID="Matt.11.6" n="6"/>“Happy is the man who does not stumble in me.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="6" osisRef="Matt.11.6" osisID="Matt.11.6!note.6">This verse is comparable to Yehovah speaking from Heaven in Matthew 3:17, and in the Cochin Matthew 3:17, it states, “...and to him,</note> The scriptures: “And blessed is he who does not stumble in Me.”<verse eID="Matt.11.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.7" osisID="Matt.11.7" n="7"/>When they went, Yeshua began to say to the Knesset (Assembly) for Yochanan (John), “Why did you go out to the dry land<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="8" osisRef="Matt.11.7" osisID="Matt.11.7!note.8">This word for “divorce” ( יגרש - yegaresh) describes one who “drives out, thrusts out, or expels” his wife.</note> to see broken reeds that are shocked by the wind?” did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed shaken by the wind?<verse eID="Matt.11.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.8" osisID="Matt.11.8" n="8"/>“And for what did you go out to see? As one clothed in mediocre clothes like this, or one of pristine? You will see it even in a house of royalty.” The scriptures: “But what did you go out to see? A man dressed in soft garments? Look, those wearing soft garments are in the houses of sovereigns.<verse eID="Matt.11.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.9" osisID="Matt.11.9" n="9"/>“Again, you went out to see a prophet? Truth I, I <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="10" osisRef="Matt.11.9" osisID="Matt.11.9!note.10">Deuteronomy 24:1.</note>say to you, that more than a prophet, is he.” The scriptures: “But what did you go out to see? A prophet? Yes, I say to you, and more than a prophet.<verse eID="Matt.11.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.10" osisID="Matt.11.10" n="10"/>“Indeed, this is he, whom it is written of him, ‘Behold, I send a messenger before you to make straight the way before you.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="11" osisRef="Matt.11.10" osisID="Matt.11.10!note.11">In Deuteronomy 24:1, the Hebrew word used for “writing” is סֵפֶר (sefer), which means, “book, scroll, writing.” However, in this verse, the author</note> The scriptures: “For this is he of whom it was written, ‘See, I send My messenger before Your face, who shall prepare Your way before You.’<verse eID="Matt.11.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.11" osisID="Matt.11.11" n="11"/>“Truth, I, I <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="13" osisRef="Matt.11.11" osisID="Matt.11.11!note.13">This is a harsh word that can mean “whore, fornicator,” or “prostitute.” The proper word for “adulterer” is נואף (no’ef).</note>say to you, ‘There has not risen among the sons of man a rabbi, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="14" osisRef="Matt.11.11" osisID="Matt.11.11!note.14">Collectively, including details from Matthew 5:32 and Luke 16:18, along with this passage, the man who marries the first wife will be an adulterer נואף (no’ef) because the woman was not divorced properly with her כריתות (keritut, “divorcement”). The woman who is separated, or rather “put away” גרושה (gerusha), is still considered married. The man in Matthew 19:9 makes himself a fornicator זונה (zonah) by taking a 2nd woman without</note>like Yochanan (John) the Purifier. And he, the smallest in heaven, is greater than him!’” The scriptures: “Truly, I say to you, among those born of women there has not risen one greater than Yoḥanan the Immerser, yet the least one in the reign of the heavens is greater than he.<verse eID="Matt.11.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.12" osisID="Matt.11.12" n="12"/>“From the birth of Yochanan (John) the Purifier and until now, the kingdom of heaven is strengthened, and all the accusers will cease and be silenced;” The scriptures: “And from the days of Yoḥanan the Immerser till now the reign of the heavens is violated, and the violent seize it.<verse eID="Matt.11.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.13" osisID="Matt.11.13" n="13"/>“according to every prophet and the Torah until the appointed time of Yochanan (John) that they prophesied. The scriptures: “For all the prophets and the Torah prophesied till Yoḥanan.<verse eID="Matt.11.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.14" osisID="Matt.11.14" n="14"/>“And if you want to receive it, he is Elyyahu (Elijah), who is intending to come.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="16" osisRef="Matt.11.14" osisID="Matt.11.14!note.16">In Matthew 16:19, Yeshua is speaking directly to Peter about building an assembly upon the stone of truth that Yeshua is the Messiah by saying, “To you I will give the keys of heaven and all of what you prohibit ( אוסר oser) in the world will be prohibited in the heavens, and all of what remains will be permitted in the heaven.” However, in Matthew 18:18, Yeshua is speaking directly to all the disciples about handling conflict in the community by saying, “...all what is bound ( קשור kishur) on earth, is bound in the heavens: and what is opened on earth will be opened in the heavens.” These are speaking of two different truths: 1. The truth that Yeshua is the Messiah is the foundation of the new covenant assembly. However, if “the heavens” is an idiom of “heavenly beings,” according to Joseph A. Fitzmeyer’s analysis of the 1350 Hittite treaty (See the English Edition of the Aramaic text, with references to biblical parallels, in Joseph A. Fitzmyer, trans., “The Inscriptions of Bar-Ga’yah and Mati’el from Sefire,” The Context of Scripture 2.82 (W. Hallo and K. L. Younger, eds. [Leiden: Brill, 2000]), 213–215), then the keys to the heavens appear to be keys that permit or prohibit heavenly beings in situations such as when an individual is turned over the satan for destruction of the flesh and correction when Paul turned two individuals over to Satan in 1 Corinthians 5:5, and 1 Timothy 1:20. Even though Yeshua was speaking directly to Peter, and Paul, who was not a disciple of Yeshua but was sent by Yeshua, was given LEGAL AUTHORITY over the assemblies was able to turn these men over to Satan. This can also be done by those who have legal authority over the assembly, but it is recommended that you ask Yehovah to fight the fight on their behalf, other than attempting to face off with demonic entities. 2. In Matthew 18:18, the disciples are taught how to resolve conflicts and to be humble with a forgiving heart. This verse seems to indicate that “all what is bound,” meaning either by man or God, is bound in the heavens. Likewise, all that is opened on earth, by man or God, is opened in the heavens. Therefore, if a person “binds” something on earth, such as a spiritual being, then it is bound in the heavens. But if something is “opened” on earth — like a modern television, radio, internet, or a cursed item — then it is opened upon the</note> The scriptures: “And if you wish to accept it, he is Ěliyahu who was about to come.<verse eID="Matt.11.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.15" osisID="Matt.11.15" n="15"/>“Whoever of him has ears to hear, let him hear.” The scriptures: “He who has ears to hear, let him hear!<verse eID="Matt.11.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.16" osisID="Matt.11.16" n="16"/>“To whom will I compare these kindred? This resembles little ones sitting in the market and calling out to their friends,” The scriptures: “And to what shall I compare this generation? It is like children sitting in the market- places and calling to their companions,<verse eID="Matt.11.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.17" osisID="Matt.11.17" n="17"/>“and they say, 'We sing for you and you are not dancing. And we lament for you, but you do not weep.’” The scriptures: and saying, ‘We played the flute for you, and you did not dance; we lamented to you, and you did not beat the breast.’<verse eID="Matt.11.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.18" osisID="Matt.11.18" n="18"/>“Because of this, Yochanan (John) saw and came not for eating and not drinking, and they<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="17" osisRef="Matt.11.18" osisID="Matt.11.18!note.17">See Isaiah 6:9–10.</note> said, ‘A demon possessed him.’” The scriptures: “For Yoḥanan came neither eating nor drinking, and they say, ‘He has a demon.’<verse eID="Matt.11.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.19" osisID="Matt.11.19" n="19"/>“The Son of man came, ate, and drank, and he says, ‘You will see this man is gluttonous, and a drunkard, and he loves lawlessness and sinners.’ And the wisdom<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="18" osisRef="Matt.11.19" osisID="Matt.11.19!note.18">The Nit’pael verb binyan is a mix of the Nif’al and Hit’pael verb binyanim and was used regularly in Mishnaic Hebrew to express a</note> of his righteousness is by works.” The scriptures: “The Son of Ada̱m came eating and drinking, and they say, ‘See, a man, a glutton and a winedrinker, a friend of tax collectors and sinners!’ And wisdom was declared right by her works.”<verse eID="Matt.11.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.20" osisID="Matt.11.20" n="20"/>Then Yeshua began to rebuke those provinces which had done many mighty things, and they did not do repentance. The scriptures: Then He began to reproach the cities in which most of His miracles had been done, because they did not repent:<verse eID="Matt.11.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.21" osisID="Matt.11.21" n="21"/>And he h̶e said, “Woe to you, Korazin (Chorazin)! Woe to you, Bet Tzaidah (Bethsaida)! And if Tzor (Tyre) and Tzidon (Sidon) began doing marvelous things, they would have cried, and outside of this, they would have done repentance in sack<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="20" osisRef="Matt.11.21" osisID="Matt.11.21!note.20">This word is first found in Mishnah Avodah Zara 5:3.</note> and ashes!” The scriptures: “Woe to you, Korazin! Woe to you, Bĕyth Tsaida̱! Because if the miracles which were done in you had started this in Tsor and Tsido̱n, they would have repented long ago in sackcloth and ashes.<verse eID="Matt.11.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.22" osisID="Matt.11.22" n="22"/>“In truth, I I̶ say to you, for Tzor (Tyre) and Tzidon (Sidon) there will be a restful spirit on the day of judgement, but not for you!” The scriptures: “But I say to you, it shall be more bearable for Tsor and Tsido̱n in the day of judgment than for you.<verse eID="Matt.11.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.23" osisID="Matt.11.23" n="23"/>“And you Kephar Nahum (Capernaum), ‘from the heavens would you be cast? For down to Sheol, you will be lowered. For which in Sodom they had seen as these marvelous things, as were among them, as before.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="21" osisRef="Matt.11.23" osisID="Matt.11.23!note.21">Literally, “he/it lent.” It appears the author intended to write it as a noun, but wrote as a verb.</note> The scriptures: “And you, Kephar Naḥum, who were exalted to the heaven, shall be brought down to She’ol! Because if the miracles which were done in you had been done in Sedo̱m, it would have remained until this day.<verse eID="Matt.11.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.24" osisID="Matt.11.24" n="24"/>“In truth, I say to you, for Sodom there will be a restful spirit on the day of judgment, but not for you.” The scriptures: “But I say to you that it shall be more bearable for the land of Sedo̱m in the day of judgment than for you.”<verse eID="Matt.11.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.25" osisID="Matt.11.25" n="25"/>Then Yeshua said in that hour, saying, “I give thanks before You, my Father, who is in the heavens, and earth. For You have concealed these words from the wise and intelligent, and likened them to children.” earth, because You have hidden these matters from clever and learned ones and have revealed them to babes.<verse eID="Matt.11.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.26" osisID="Matt.11.26" n="26"/>“If so, my Father, Your desire be before You.” The scriptures: “Yes, Father, because so it was well-pleasing in Your sight.<verse eID="Matt.11.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.27" osisID="Matt.11.27" n="27"/>“All what is delivered to me of my Father, and no man knows His Son, only his Father; also no man knows the Father only His Son, and he to whom the Son wants to rebel.” The scriptures: “All have been handed over to Me by My Father, and no one knows the Son except the Father. Nor does anyone know the Father except the Son, and he to whom the Son wishes to reveal Him.<verse eID="Matt.11.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.28" osisID="Matt.11.28" n="28"/>“Come near to me, all of you in yokes of hardship, and with yokes of burdens, for I will give you rest.” The scriptures: “Come to Me, all you who labour and are burdened, and I shall give you rest.<verse eID="Matt.11.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.29" osisID="Matt.11.29" n="29"/>“You are to carry my yoke upon you and learn from me, for I am stricken of spirit and young of the heart, and you will find rest for your spirit and souls.” The scriptures: “Take My yoke upon you and learn from Me, for I am meek and humble in heart, and you shall find rest for your beings<verse eID="Matt.11.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.11.30" osisID="Matt.11.30" n="30"/>“For my yoke is from the inside, and my burden is light.” The scriptures: “For My yoke is gentle and My burden is light.’”<verse eID="Matt.11.30"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.11"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.12" osisID="Matt.12" n="12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.1" osisID="Matt.12.1" n="1"/>At that time, Yeshua walked on the Sabbath in the seed house,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="4" osisRef="Matt.12.1" osisID="Matt.12.1!note.4">The pronoun for “he” or “it” is an added pronoun as it is included in the word ענה (ana, “he/it answered).. It is possible the author intended to make it present tense.</note> and his talmidim (students) were hungry and began collecting ears of grain and eating.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="5" osisRef="Matt.12.1" osisID="Matt.12.1!note.5">Genesis 1:27.</note><verse eID="Matt.12.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.2" osisID="Matt.12.2" n="2"/>The Pharisees, when they saw this, said to him, “Behold! Your talmidim (students) are doing something that is forbidden to do on the Sabbath.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="6" osisRef="Matt.12.2" osisID="Matt.12.2!note.6">This verse is comparable to Yehovah speaking from Heaven in Matthew 3:17, and in the Cochin Matthew 3:17, it states, “...and to him,</note><verse eID="Matt.12.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.3" osisID="Matt.12.3" n="3"/>Then he replied to them, “Have you not read what David did, when he was hungry, and the men that were with him?”<verse eID="Matt.12.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.4" osisID="Matt.12.4" n="4"/>“How he entered into the house of Elohim (God) and ate the bread of the presence (table of showbread) which was not appropriate for him to eat nor for the men who were with him, but rather for the priests alone.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="7" osisRef="Matt.12.4" osisID="Matt.12.4!note.7">See Exodus 21:17 and Leviticus 20:9.</note><verse eID="Matt.12.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.5" osisID="Matt.12.5" n="5"/>“And again, have you not read in the Torah that the priests in the holy place profane on the Sabbath, and they were not afraid or sinning from this?”<verse eID="Matt.12.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.6" osisID="Matt.12.6" n="6"/>“I ‒ I say to you that one greater than the holy place is here.”<verse eID="Matt.12.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.7" osisID="Matt.12.7" n="7"/>“The following matter you are to know; whoever my father pardons, I do not want sacrifice or peace offerings.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="9" osisRef="Matt.12.7" osisID="Matt.12.7!note.9">Or may also mean, “against you.” It also appears that the scribe may not have intended to write the word אני (ani, “I”), with the middle letter being the letter nun, but instead, he may have intended to write the word אוי (oy, “Woe!), with the middle letter being a vav. Thus, if</note> He (Yehovah) will not demonstrate love for those who do not fear Him.”<verse eID="Matt.12.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.8" osisID="Matt.12.8" n="8"/>“Lord of the Sabbath is he, the Son of man.”<verse eID="Matt.12.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.9" osisID="Matt.12.9" n="9"/>And Yeshua departed from there and came in the midst of their community.<verse eID="Matt.12.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.10" osisID="Matt.12.10" n="10"/>And there was a man whose hand withered, and they asked him if he is able to heal on the Sabbath so that he would be destroyed by it.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="10" osisRef="Matt.12.10" osisID="Matt.12.10!note.10">Deuteronomy 24:1.</note><verse eID="Matt.12.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.11" osisID="Matt.12.11" n="11"/>This one responded and said, “Who among you is a man that has a sheep that has fallen into a pit on the Sabbath, would not raise it out?”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="11" osisRef="Matt.12.11" osisID="Matt.12.11!note.11">In Deuteronomy 24:1, the Hebrew word used for “writing” is סֵפֶר (sefer), which means, “book, scroll, writing.” However, in this verse, the author</note><verse eID="Matt.12.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.12" osisID="Matt.12.12" n="12"/>“How much more is a son of man than a sheep! By this judgement, it is appropriate on the Sabbath to do good.”<verse eID="Matt.12.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.13" osisID="Matt.12.13" n="13"/>And hastily he said to the man, “Stretch out your hand.” And he stretched out, and it became well like its accompanying hand.<verse eID="Matt.12.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.14" osisID="Matt.12.14" n="14"/>And the Pharisees and the king went out, and they arose against him to destroy him.<verse eID="Matt.12.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.15" osisID="Matt.12.15" n="15"/>Then Yeshua, when he knew this, went after the crowds and healed all of them.<verse eID="Matt.12.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.16" osisID="Matt.12.16" n="16"/>And he would remind them not to reveal him,<verse eID="Matt.12.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.17" osisID="Matt.12.17" n="17"/>to carry out what was said through <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="12" osisRef="Matt.12.17" osisID="Matt.12.17!note.12">Literal meanings: “drives out, thrusts, expels.”</note>the prophet Yeshayah (Isaiah),<verse eID="Matt.12.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.18" osisID="Matt.12.18" n="18"/>“Behold, my servant whom I support, my chosen one whom my soul delights. I have given my Spirit upon him, and judgment to the nations, he will bring out.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="14" osisRef="Matt.12.18" osisID="Matt.12.18!note.14">Collectively, including details from Matthew 5:32 and Luke 16:18, along with this passage, the man who marries the first wife will be an adulterer נואף (no’ef) because the woman was not divorced properly with her כריתות (keritut, “divorcement”). The woman who is separated, or rather “put away” גרושה (gerusha), is still considered married. The man in Matthew 19:9 makes himself a fornicator זונה (zonah) by taking a 2nd woman without</note><verse eID="Matt.12.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.19" osisID="Matt.12.19" n="19"/>“He will not shout, or lift up, or cause his voice to be heard on the outside.”<verse eID="Matt.12.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.20" osisID="Matt.12.20" n="20"/>“A crushed reed, he will not break, and a darkened wick he will not extinguish, until deliver judgment unto truth.”<verse eID="Matt.12.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.21" osisID="Matt.12.21" n="21"/>“And in his name, they, the people, were hoping.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="15" osisRef="Matt.12.21" osisID="Matt.12.21!note.15">Can also be translated to, “men with a woman.”</note><verse eID="Matt.12.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.22" osisID="Matt.12.22" n="22"/>After this, they brought him one madman, who was also mute and blind, and he healed him, so the mute and blind spoke and saw.<verse eID="Matt.12.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.23" osisID="Matt.12.23" n="23"/>And all the community was amazed. And they said, “Perhaps this is the son of David?”<verse eID="Matt.12.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.24" osisID="Matt.12.24" n="24"/>When the Pharisees heard, they said, “Certainly he does not expel demons except in the place of Ba’al Zebub (Be’elzubul), the chief of the demons.”<verse eID="Matt.12.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.25" osisID="Matt.12.25" n="25"/>When he understood their thoughts, he said to them, “Every kingdom that is divided against itself is destroyed, and every house and province that you divide against itself cannot exist.”<verse eID="Matt.12.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.26" osisID="Matt.12.26" n="26"/>“And if Satan brings an accusation against himself and makes division; for however can his kingdom stand?”<verse eID="Matt.12.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.27" osisID="Matt.12.27" n="27"/>“And if I expel by Ba’al Zebub (Beelzebub), the demons, why would your children expel him in haste? So therefore, they will be defendants against you.”<verse eID="Matt.12.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.28" osisID="Matt.12.28" n="28"/>“And if I expel the demons by the power of the Ruach (Spirit) of Elohim (God) of demons, then the kingdom of heaven has come near upon you.”<verse eID="Matt.12.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.29" osisID="Matt.12.29" n="29"/>“How can a common man enter into the house of one man, a mighty one, and steal his mammon (money), but first needing to bind the mighty one, and then his house will be stolen?”<verse eID="Matt.12.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.30" osisID="Matt.12.30" n="30"/>“Whoever is not with me, I become jealous, and whoever does not assemble with me is surely scattered.”<verse eID="Matt.12.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.31" osisID="Matt.12.31" n="31"/>“Therefore, I say to you that all sins and contemptible things can be forgiven for the sons of man. However, contemptible things that are against the Spirit will not be forgiven.”<verse eID="Matt.12.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.32" osisID="Matt.12.32" n="32"/>“And everyone who says a word about the Son of man, it will be forgiven of him. But whoever says about the Holy Spirit, it is not forgiven of him; not in this world or in the coming world<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="27" osisRef="Matt.12.32" osisID="Matt.12.32!note.27">This word is first used in the Mishnah Berakhot 2:5, “to refrain.”</note>.”<verse eID="Matt.12.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.33" osisID="Matt.12.33" n="33"/>“Either you make a tree good and its fruit good, or you make the tree bad and its fruit bad; from its fruit you shall know the tree.”<verse eID="Matt.12.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.34" osisID="Matt.12.34" n="34"/>“Children of serpents! How are you able to speak good, but you are evil? From the intentions<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="28" osisRef="Matt.12.34" osisID="Matt.12.34!note.28">Can have a common plural meaning, “people.”</note> of your heart, your mouth speaks.”<verse eID="Matt.12.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.35" osisID="Matt.12.35" n="35"/>“A good man, from his good thinking, brings out good. And an evil man, from his evil thinking, brings evil.”<verse eID="Matt.12.35"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.36" osisID="Matt.12.36" n="36"/>“I say to you that all idle words that they, the sons of mankind, are hanged by their words on the day of judgment.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="29" osisRef="Matt.12.36" osisID="Matt.12.36!note.29">This word is first found in the Mishnah Tractate Sanhedrin 6:4.</note><verse eID="Matt.12.36"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.37" osisID="Matt.12.37" n="37"/>“From your words, you will be justified, and by your words, you will be condemned.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="33" osisRef="Matt.12.37" osisID="Matt.12.37!note.33">Literally, “indebted to.”</note>”<verse eID="Matt.12.37"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.38" osisID="Matt.12.38" n="38"/>Then the people from the scribes and Pharisees answered, “Our Rabbi, we ‒ we need to see some sign from you.”<verse eID="Matt.12.38"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.39" osisID="Matt.12.39" n="39"/>Then he answered and said, “A wicked and adulterous kindred seek signs, but the sign will not be given, except a sign of Yonah (Jonah), the prophet.”<verse eID="Matt.12.39"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.40" osisID="Matt.12.40" n="40"/>“As Yonah (Jonah) who was in the inward parts of the fish, three days and three nights,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="37" osisRef="Matt.12.40" osisID="Matt.12.40!note.37">Literally, “while he is with him.”</note> in this way, the Son of man will be in the heart of the earth three days and three nights.”<verse eID="Matt.12.40"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.41" osisID="Matt.12.41" n="41"/>“My people will rise up with this kindred and charge them, for they repented with the preaching<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="38" osisRef="Matt.12.41" osisID="Matt.12.41!note.38">Literally, “proclamation” or “declaration.”</note> of Yonah (Jonah); and here is one greater than Yonah (Yonah).”<verse eID="Matt.12.41"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.42" osisID="Matt.12.42" n="42"/>“The kingdom of the South will rise in the judgement with this kinsmen and charge it, for she came from a distant land to hear the wisdom of Shelomoh (Solomon), and here is one greater than Shelomoh (Solomon).”<verse eID="Matt.12.42"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.43" osisID="Matt.12.43" n="43"/>“Whenever the impure spirit goes out from the sons of man, it goes to a restful place to rest and does not find it.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="41" osisRef="Matt.12.43" osisID="Matt.12.43!note.41">In this verse, the demons are referred to as “he,” and in the following two verses, they are referenced as “she.”</note><verse eID="Matt.12.43"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.44" osisID="Matt.12.44" n="44"/>Then she says, “I will overthrow my house from which I went out.” And she comes and finds the house empty and turns upside down.”<verse eID="Matt.12.44"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.45" osisID="Matt.12.45" n="45"/>“Then they go and take with it seven other spirits more evil <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="44" osisRef="Matt.12.45" osisID="Matt.12.45!note.44">Can also translate to “eviler.”</note>than itself, and they come and dwell in the same house, and it became worse for that person than at the beginning; and thus will it be for these evil kindred.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="45" osisRef="Matt.12.45" osisID="Matt.12.45!note.45">This can also be translated as “and so will it be for this evil generation,” as this is speaking of a different group of family members at a</note><verse eID="Matt.12.45"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.46" osisID="Matt.12.46" n="46"/>When he was speaking this, his mother and brothers came and stood outside and wanted to speak with him.<verse eID="Matt.12.46"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.47" osisID="Matt.12.47" n="47"/>One man said to him, “Behold, your mother and brothers stand outside and want to speak with you.”<verse eID="Matt.12.47"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.48" osisID="Matt.12.48" n="48"/>He, this one, responded to whoever told him, “Who is it, my mother, and who are they, my brothers?”<verse eID="Matt.12.48"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.49" osisID="Matt.12.49" n="49"/>And he sent his hand toward his talmidim (students) and said, “These ‒ they are my mother and they are my brothers!”<verse eID="Matt.12.49"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.12.50" osisID="Matt.12.50" n="50"/>“Whoever does the desire of my Father, he is my brother, and my sisters, and my mother.”<verse eID="Matt.12.50"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.12"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.13" osisID="Matt.13" n="13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.1" osisID="Matt.13.1" n="1"/>On the same day that Yeshua went out from his house, he sat upon the riverbank of the sea.<verse eID="Matt.13.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.2" osisID="Matt.13.2" n="2"/>Many people assembled and drew near him. When he went out, he sat in the ship, and all the people stood on the riverbank of the sea.<verse eID="Matt.13.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.3" osisID="Matt.13.3" n="3"/>And he spoke many words with them in segments <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="5" osisRef="Matt.13.3" osisID="Matt.13.3!note.5">Genesis 1:27.</note>and in Proverbs. Then he said this, “The sower went to sow,”<verse eID="Matt.13.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.4" osisID="Matt.13.4" n="4"/>“and when he sowed this, a few fell on the road, birds came and ate them.”<verse eID="Matt.13.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.5" osisID="Matt.13.5" n="5"/>“And one portion fell on the hills in a place that without much dirt on it. And in that hour, it grew because there was no dirt for its root.”<verse eID="Matt.13.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.6" osisID="Matt.13.6" n="6"/>“And when the sun shone on it and it became heated, because it had no root for it, it withered.”<verse eID="Matt.13.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.7" osisID="Matt.13.7" n="7"/>“And one portion fell in the place of the thorns, and when it grew, the thorns choked it.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="9" osisRef="Matt.13.7" osisID="Matt.13.7!note.9">Or may also mean, “against you.” It also appears that the scribe may not have intended to write the word אני (ani, “I”), with the middle letter being the letter nun, but instead, he may have intended to write the word אוי (oy, “Woe!), with the middle letter being a vav. Thus, if</note><verse eID="Matt.13.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.8" osisID="Matt.13.8" n="8"/>“And one portion fell on good land and produced fruit; some had given a hundred, some sixty, and some thirty.”<verse eID="Matt.13.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.9" osisID="Matt.13.9" n="9"/>“Whoever has ears to hear, he will hear.”<verse eID="Matt.13.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.10" osisID="Matt.13.10" n="10"/>And his talmidim (students) approached him, “Why do you speak parables with them?”<verse eID="Matt.13.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.11" osisID="Matt.13.11" n="11"/>“Again, he <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="10" osisRef="Matt.13.11" osisID="Matt.13.11!note.10">Deuteronomy 24:1.</note>answered and said, “To you is given to know the mystery of the kingdom of heaven, but for those it is not given.”<verse eID="Matt.13.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.12" osisID="Matt.13.12" n="12"/>“To whoever has, they give to him, and it increases more so. But whoever does not have, even what he has, they take from him.”<verse eID="Matt.13.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.13" osisID="Matt.13.13" n="13"/>“Because of this, I <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="12" osisRef="Matt.13.13" osisID="Matt.13.13!note.12">Literal meanings: “drives out, thrusts, expels.”</note>speak with them in a parable, for they see, and do not see. <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="13" osisRef="Matt.13.13" osisID="Matt.13.13!note.13">This is a harsh word that can mean “whore, fornicator,” or “prostitute.” The proper word for “adulterer” is נואף (no’ef).</note>And hearing they do not hear; and as well, they do not believe.”<verse eID="Matt.13.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.14" osisID="Matt.13.14" n="14"/>“And by them, the prophecy of Yeshayah (Isaiah) is validated, that said, ‘Hearing, you hear and you don’t understand, and seeing you see and you don’t perceive,<verse eID="Matt.13.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.15" osisID="Matt.13.15" n="15"/>“The heart of this people is fat, their ears are heavy, and their eyes shut, lest they will see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their heart, and they return<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="16" osisRef="Matt.13.15" osisID="Matt.13.15!note.16">In Matthew 16:19, Yeshua is speaking directly to Peter about building an assembly upon the stone of truth that Yeshua is the Messiah by saying, “To you I will give the keys of heaven and all of what you prohibit ( אוסר oser) in the world will be prohibited in the heavens, and all of what remains will be permitted in the heaven.” However, in Matthew 18:18, Yeshua is speaking directly to all the disciples about handling conflict in the community by saying, “...all what is bound ( קשור kishur) on earth, is bound in the heavens: and what is opened on earth will be opened in the heavens.” These are speaking of two different truths: 1. The truth that Yeshua is the Messiah is the foundation of the new covenant assembly. However, if “the heavens” is an idiom of “heavenly beings,” according to Joseph A. Fitzmeyer’s analysis of the 1350 Hittite treaty (See the English Edition of the Aramaic text, with references to biblical parallels, in Joseph A. Fitzmyer, trans., “The Inscriptions of Bar-Ga’yah and Mati’el from Sefire,” The Context of Scripture 2.82 (W. Hallo and K. L. Younger, eds. [Leiden: Brill, 2000]), 213–215), then the keys to the heavens appear to be keys that permit or prohibit heavenly beings in situations such as when an individual is turned over the satan for destruction of the flesh and correction when Paul turned two individuals over to Satan in 1 Corinthians 5:5, and 1 Timothy 1:20. Even though Yeshua was speaking directly to Peter, and Paul, who was not a disciple of Yeshua but was sent by Yeshua, was given LEGAL AUTHORITY over the assemblies was able to turn these men over to Satan. This can also be done by those who have legal authority over the assembly, but it is recommended that you ask Yehovah to fight the fight on their behalf, other than attempting to face off with demonic entities. 2. In Matthew 18:18, the disciples are taught how to resolve conflicts and to be humble with a forgiving heart. This verse seems to indicate that “all what is bound,” meaning either by man or God, is bound in the heavens. Likewise, all that is opened on earth, by man or God, is opened in the heavens. Therefore, if a person “binds” something on earth, such as a spiritual being, then it is bound in the heavens. But if something is “opened” on earth — like a modern television, radio, internet, or a cursed item — then it is opened upon the</note> and are healed.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="17" osisRef="Matt.13.15" osisID="Matt.13.15!note.17">See Isaiah 6:9–10.</note><verse eID="Matt.13.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.16" osisID="Matt.13.16" n="16"/>“On you is the goodness for your eyes see and your ears hear.”<verse eID="Matt.13.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.17" osisID="Matt.13.17" n="17"/>“In truth, for I <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="23" osisRef="Matt.13.17" osisID="Matt.13.17!note.23">This appears to be a misspelled word and should be תִּלְּמְדו “you will learn.” It appears to be a defective spelling of “you (mp) will learn.”</note>say to you that many prophets and righteous ones longed to see what you see, but did not see, and to hear what you hear, but did not hear.”<verse eID="Matt.13.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.18" osisID="Matt.13.18" n="18"/>“You - you <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="24" osisRef="Matt.13.18" osisID="Matt.13.18!note.24">Literally, “If it is you, command that I, he will come…” Thus,</note>will understand the proverb of the sower.”<verse eID="Matt.13.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.19" osisID="Matt.13.19" n="19"/>“All who hear the words of the kingdom and do not discern it, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="25" osisRef="Matt.13.19" osisID="Matt.13.19!note.25">The word קדמונים is the Hebrew equivalent of the Aramaic ܐܝܡܕܩ (qaddimāya), meaning the “first ones” from the ancient world. While accurately</note>and had sown it without desire.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="26" osisRef="Matt.13.19" osisID="Matt.13.19!note.26">Aramaic word that is a metaphor for making it rain. See Aph Dem 4.65:25: ܐܪܛܡ ܬܚܢܘ.</note> This is he, who sows upon the road.”<verse eID="Matt.13.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.20" osisID="Matt.13.20" n="20"/>“He who sows upon a mountain, is he who hears the word, and receives it with joy, in that same hour.”<verse eID="Matt.13.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.21" osisID="Matt.13.21" n="21"/>“He has no essence of himself, except for a time; and when there is sickness or some weakness, quickly he stumbles.”<verse eID="Matt.13.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.22" osisID="Matt.13.22" n="22"/>“That which is sown in the place of the thorns <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="27" osisRef="Matt.13.22" osisID="Matt.13.22!note.27">This word is first used in the Mishnah Berakhot 2:5, “to refrain.”</note>is he; he whom hears the word, but thought about it, in the pondering of this world, and wealth misled him and strangled the word, and it did not produce fruit.”<verse eID="Matt.13.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.23" osisID="Matt.13.23" n="23"/>And whoever sows on good earth, it is he who hears and understands, and bears <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="28" osisRef="Matt.13.23" osisID="Matt.13.23!note.28">Can have a common plural meaning, “people.”</note>fruit to a hundred or sixty, or thirty.”<verse eID="Matt.13.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.24" osisID="Matt.13.24" n="24"/>Another proverb, he compared to them, “The kingdom of heaven is like a man who sowed good seed in his city.”<verse eID="Matt.13.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.25" osisID="Matt.13.25" n="25"/>“And when they were sleeping, enemies came and sowed a weed between the wheat, and left.”<verse eID="Matt.13.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.26" osisID="Matt.13.26" n="26"/>“And when the herbs grew beautifully and they produced fruit, in that hour, appeared the weed.”<verse eID="Matt.13.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.27" osisID="Matt.13.27" n="27"/>“And the servants approached the Lord and said to him, ‘Our Lord, you sow good seed in your field. Where has the weed come from?’”<verse eID="Matt.13.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.28" osisID="Matt.13.28" n="28"/>“The Lord said to him, ‘My enemies did this.’ And his servants said, ‘Do you want to quickly remove them?’”<verse eID="Matt.13.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.29" osisID="Matt.13.29" n="29"/>“He said to them, ‘Perhaps when you remove the weed, you will uproot the wheat likewise.’”<verse eID="Matt.13.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.30" osisID="Matt.13.30" n="30"/>“But let them remain growing together, until the season of the harvest. In the hour of the harvest, I<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="33" osisRef="Matt.13.30" osisID="Matt.13.30!note.33">Literally, “indebted to.”</note> say to the reaper, ‘Cleanse first the weed, and bind them to burn. But the wheat, bring into the treasure.’<verse eID="Matt.13.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.31" osisID="Matt.13.31" n="31"/>“Another proverb I will liken for you; the kingdom of heaven resembles seeds of mustard grains that a man took and sowed in his field,”<verse eID="Matt.13.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.32" osisID="Matt.13.32" n="32"/>“and it is the smallest seed of all the seeds, but it grows from all the green herbs and becomes like a tree until the birds of the sky come to gather in its shade.”<verse eID="Matt.13.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.33" osisID="Matt.13.33" n="33"/>“Another parable I say to you, ‘The kingdom of heaven is like leaven that a woman took and planted in three measures of flour,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="35" osisRef="Matt.13.33" osisID="Matt.13.33!note.35">The vav mater lectionis is included to represent the "u" sound due to the fusion of Hebrew and Aramaic languages, although the word</note> until it leavened<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="36" osisRef="Matt.13.33" osisID="Matt.13.33!note.36">This word ( דינך ) is a legal term indicating a court of law and a legal case presented by an accuser and is usually seen in poetry. See Jeremiah 30:13.</note> all of it.’”<verse eID="Matt.13.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.34" osisID="Matt.13.34" n="34"/>“All of this was in parables for the communities, and whatever was without a parable and allegory, he did not speak with them.”<verse eID="Matt.13.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.35" osisID="Matt.13.35" n="35"/>“As it was said by the hand of the prophet, ‘I will open my mouth in a parable, I will utter dark sayings<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="37" osisRef="Matt.13.35" osisID="Matt.13.35!note.37">Literally, “while he is with him.”</note> of old.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="38" osisRef="Matt.13.35" osisID="Matt.13.35!note.38">Literally, “proclamation” or “declaration.”</note><verse eID="Matt.13.35"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.36" osisID="Matt.13.36" n="36"/>“Then Yeshua left the community and came to a house, and his talmidim (students) approached and said, “Solve the matter for us regarding the weed and the earth.”<verse eID="Matt.13.36"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.37" osisID="Matt.13.37" n="37"/>Yeshua answered and said, “The one who sowed good seed, they are sons of man.”<verse eID="Matt.13.37"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.38" osisID="Matt.13.38" n="38"/>“And the earth is the world, the good seed are children, the provision is that which has children of evil in it.”<verse eID="Matt.13.38"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.39" osisID="Matt.13.39" n="39"/>“And the enemies who sowed them are demons, his harvest is repentance and good works of the world, and the harvesters are the messengers.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="41" osisRef="Matt.13.39" osisID="Matt.13.39!note.41">In this verse, the demons are referred to as “he,” and in the following two verses, they are referenced as “she.”</note><verse eID="Matt.13.39"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.40" osisID="Matt.13.40" n="40"/>“As they grasp the nourishment and burn with fire, so will be the fulfillment <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="42" osisRef="Matt.13.40" osisID="Matt.13.40!note.42">See Mishan Gittin 4:4.</note>of this world.”<verse eID="Matt.13.40"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.41" osisID="Matt.13.41" n="41"/>“He sends out the sons of man with his messengers to select <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="43" osisRef="Matt.13.41" osisID="Matt.13.41!note.43">Can mean to “profess” or “support.”</note>from every kingdom, the stumbling blocks and all those who do unrighteousness.”<verse eID="Matt.13.41"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.42" osisID="Matt.13.42" n="42"/>“And they will throw them into the furnace of fire, and there they will be weeping and gnashing their teeth.”<verse eID="Matt.13.42"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.43" osisID="Matt.13.43" n="43"/>“Then the righteous will shine like the brightness of the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Whoever has ears to hear, he will hear.”<verse eID="Matt.13.43"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.44" osisID="Matt.13.44" n="44"/>“Again, the kingdom of heaven is as a hidden treasure <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="45" osisRef="Matt.13.44" osisID="Matt.13.44!note.45">This can also be translated as “and so will it be for this evil generation,” as this is speaking of a different group of family members at a</note>in the earth, and when a man found it, he was joyful, and went out and hid it in his place, and from joy, he went and sold everything of what he had, and bought this land.”<verse eID="Matt.13.44"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.45" osisID="Matt.13.45" n="45"/>“Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant who seeks a good pearl.”<verse eID="Matt.13.45"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.46" osisID="Matt.13.46" n="46"/>“And when he found one pearl, he went and sold all he had, and bought it.”<verse eID="Matt.13.46"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.47" osisID="Matt.13.47" n="47"/>“Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net that falls into the water and all of what fell into it, gathering fish.”<verse eID="Matt.13.47"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.48" osisID="Matt.13.48" n="48"/>“So, therefore, he raised them to dry land, full of fish. And he sat over it to sort out, and the good he threw into containers, and the different bad ones he left out(side).”<verse eID="Matt.13.48"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.49" osisID="Matt.13.49" n="49"/>“So will it be at the end of the world;<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="50" osisRef="Matt.13.49" osisID="Matt.13.49!note.50">This word is first found in Mishnah Tractate Sanhedrin (c.190 – c.230 CE).</note> the messengers will go out and divide the evil from the righteous.”<verse eID="Matt.13.49"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.50" osisID="Matt.13.50" n="50"/>“And he will throw them into the furnace into the fire; and they will be weeping and gnashing their teeth.”<verse eID="Matt.13.50"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.51" osisID="Matt.13.51" n="51"/>Yeshua said to them, “Do you understand all this?” And they said to him, “Yes, our teacher.”<verse eID="Matt.13.51"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.52" osisID="Matt.13.52" n="52"/>He said to them, “Because of this, all scribes that are taught in the kingdom of heaven resemble a man, the owner of a house, who brings out from his treasure chest, new and old.”<verse eID="Matt.13.52"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.53" osisID="Matt.13.53" n="53"/>Then, when Yeshua completed these parables, he changed his location from there.<verse eID="Matt.13.53"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.54" osisID="Matt.13.54" n="54"/>And he came to a district and taught them in their community. How they were amazed, saying, “So where does he have this wisdom and wonders?”<verse eID="Matt.13.54"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.55" osisID="Matt.13.55" n="55"/>“Is not this the son of a carpenter of wood? Is not his mother called Miryam, and his brothers Ya’akov (Jacob), Yoseph (Joseph), Shim’on (Simon), and Yehudah (Judah)?”<verse eID="Matt.13.55"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.56" osisID="Matt.13.56" n="56"/>“And also, his sisters, are they not with them? From where come to him all this?”<verse eID="Matt.13.56"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.57" osisID="Matt.13.57" n="57"/>And they were amazed by him. So, Yeshua said to them, “There is no prophet without honor, only in his city and in his house.”<verse eID="Matt.13.57"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.13.58" osisID="Matt.13.58" n="58"/>Then Yeshua went out and did not do great marvelous wonders <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="53" osisRef="Matt.13.58" osisID="Matt.13.58!note.53">The spelling of this word is most unusual. The pronominal suffix ends with what would normally be a third masculine singular pronominal suffix for a noun, not a verb. However, it is clear this is a verb and the root is ( כלא ). In Hebrew it is כָּלָּא (ka’la) and in Aramaic it is כְּלָּא (ke’la). But it is clear this is a word that has been influenced by the Aramaic language.</note>there because they did not believe in him.<verse eID="Matt.13.58"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.13"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.14" osisID="Matt.14" n="14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.1" osisID="Matt.14.1" n="1"/>In that very hour, Horodes (Herodes) the tetrarch <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="4" osisRef="Matt.14.1" osisID="Matt.14.1!note.4">The pronoun for “he” or “it” is an added pronoun as it is included in the word ענה (ana, “he/it answered).. It is possible the author intended to make it present tense.</note>(including <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="5" osisRef="Matt.14.1" osisID="Matt.14.1!note.5">Genesis 1:27.</note>Horodos (Herodes) the fourth) heard of Yeshua.<verse eID="Matt.14.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.2" osisID="Matt.14.2" n="2"/>And he said to his servants, “Yochanan (John) the Purifier has risen from the dead, because of this, he does these marvelous things.”<verse eID="Matt.14.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.3" osisID="Matt.14.3" n="3"/>This Horodes (Herodes) seized Yochanan, and bound him, and threw him into the house of prisoners, because of Herodiah (Herodias), the wife of Philippos (Philip), his brother.<verse eID="Matt.14.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.4" osisID="Matt.14.4" n="4"/>Then he - indeed, Yochanan (John) said to him that he is not allowed <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="6" osisRef="Matt.14.4" osisID="Matt.14.4!note.6">This verse is comparable to Yehovah speaking from Heaven in Matthew 3:17, and in the Cochin Matthew 3:17, it states, “...and to him,</note>to take her to be his wife.<verse eID="Matt.14.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.5" osisID="Matt.14.5" n="5"/>And he wanted to kill him, but because he feared the crowds of people, he didn’t kill him from among them, for they were thinking him to be a prophet.<verse eID="Matt.14.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.6" osisID="Matt.14.6" n="6"/>When it was the feast of the birthday of Horodos (Herodes), the daughter of Herodiah (Herodias) danced before him and Horodos (Herodes) desired her.<verse eID="Matt.14.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.7" osisID="Matt.14.7" n="7"/>Because of this, with an oath he said to her, he would give her all <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="9" osisRef="Matt.14.7" osisID="Matt.14.7!note.9">Or may also mean, “against you.” It also appears that the scribe may not have intended to write the word אני (ani, “I”), with the middle letter being the letter nun, but instead, he may have intended to write the word אוי (oy, “Woe!), with the middle letter being a vav. Thus, if</note>of what she asked.<verse eID="Matt.14.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.8" osisID="Matt.14.8" n="8"/>And she, because of numerous <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="10" osisRef="Matt.14.8" osisID="Matt.14.8!note.10">Deuteronomy 24:1.</note>urges of her mother, said, “Give me now on one dish, the head belonging to Yochanan (John) the Purifier!”<verse eID="Matt.14.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.9" osisID="Matt.14.9" n="9"/>The king sighed because of her — because of the oath he made at the table, he commanded that it be given to her.<verse eID="Matt.14.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.10" osisID="Matt.14.10" n="10"/>Then he sent for and murdered <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="12" osisRef="Matt.14.10" osisID="Matt.14.10!note.12">Literal meanings: “drives out, thrusts, expels.”</note>Yochanan (John) in the house of prisoners.<verse eID="Matt.14.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.11" osisID="Matt.14.11" n="11"/>And he brought his head on a dish and gave it to the girl, and she brought it to her mother.<verse eID="Matt.14.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.12" osisID="Matt.14.12" n="12"/>And his talmidim (students) came and took his body and buried it, and they came and told Yeshua.<verse eID="Matt.14.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.13" osisID="Matt.14.13" n="13"/>When Yeshua had heard it, he changed his location in a ship <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="13" osisRef="Matt.14.13" osisID="Matt.14.13!note.13">This is a harsh word that can mean “whore, fornicator,” or “prostitute.” The proper word for “adulterer” is נואף (no’ef).</note>for a desert place to be alone. But then the congregation walked after him on dry ground from the land.<verse eID="Matt.14.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.14" osisID="Matt.14.14" n="14"/>And Yeshua went out with the community and had mercy on them. So, he healed their pains.<verse eID="Matt.14.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.15" osisID="Matt.14.15" n="15"/>When it was evening, his talmidim (students) drew near him and they said to him, “This is a dry place and the time of eating has passed. You could send the community so they can go to the city and buy themselves food.”<verse eID="Matt.14.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.16" osisID="Matt.14.16" n="16"/>He, he said to them, “They do not need to go. <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="16" osisRef="Matt.14.16" osisID="Matt.14.16!note.16">In Matthew 16:19, Yeshua is speaking directly to Peter about building an assembly upon the stone of truth that Yeshua is the Messiah by saying, “To you I will give the keys of heaven and all of what you prohibit ( אוסר oser) in the world will be prohibited in the heavens, and all of what remains will be permitted in the heaven.” However, in Matthew 18:18, Yeshua is speaking directly to all the disciples about handling conflict in the community by saying, “...all what is bound ( קשור kishur) on earth, is bound in the heavens: and what is opened on earth will be opened in the heavens.” These are speaking of two different truths: 1. The truth that Yeshua is the Messiah is the foundation of the new covenant assembly. However, if “the heavens” is an idiom of “heavenly beings,” according to Joseph A. Fitzmeyer’s analysis of the 1350 Hittite treaty (See the English Edition of the Aramaic text, with references to biblical parallels, in Joseph A. Fitzmyer, trans., “The Inscriptions of Bar-Ga’yah and Mati’el from Sefire,” The Context of Scripture 2.82 (W. Hallo and K. L. Younger, eds. [Leiden: Brill, 2000]), 213–215), then the keys to the heavens appear to be keys that permit or prohibit heavenly beings in situations such as when an individual is turned over the satan for destruction of the flesh and correction when Paul turned two individuals over to Satan in 1 Corinthians 5:5, and 1 Timothy 1:20. Even though Yeshua was speaking directly to Peter, and Paul, who was not a disciple of Yeshua but was sent by Yeshua, was given LEGAL AUTHORITY over the assemblies was able to turn these men over to Satan. This can also be done by those who have legal authority over the assembly, but it is recommended that you ask Yehovah to fight the fight on their behalf, other than attempting to face off with demonic entities. 2. In Matthew 18:18, the disciples are taught how to resolve conflicts and to be humble with a forgiving heart. This verse seems to indicate that “all what is bound,” meaning either by man or God, is bound in the heavens. Likewise, all that is opened on earth, by man or God, is opened in the heavens. Therefore, if a person “binds” something on earth, such as a spiritual being, then it is bound in the heavens. But if something is “opened” on earth — like a modern television, radio, internet, or a cursed item — then it is opened upon the</note>You are to give them food.”<verse eID="Matt.14.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.17" osisID="Matt.14.17" n="17"/>They said, “There is nothing here, but rather five loaves and two fish.”<verse eID="Matt.14.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.18" osisID="Matt.14.18" n="18"/>He said to them, “You bring them here to me.”<verse eID="Matt.14.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.19" osisID="Matt.14.19" n="19"/>And he commanded the community to sit on the earth. Then he took the five loaves <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="18" osisRef="Matt.14.19" osisID="Matt.14.19!note.18">The Nit’pael verb binyan is a mix of the Nif’al and Hit’pael verb binyanim and was used regularly in Mishnaic Hebrew to express a</note>and two fishes, and set it before them, and looked into the heavens, and blessed, and divided it to his talmidim (students). Then his talmidim (students) gave to the community,<verse eID="Matt.14.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.20" osisID="Matt.14.20" n="20"/>and all of them ate and were satisfied and they (the talmidim) took from the remaining pieces, twelve full baskets.<verse eID="Matt.14.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.21" osisID="Matt.14.21" n="21"/>This people who ate were five thousand, besides <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="19" osisRef="Matt.14.21" osisID="Matt.14.21!note.19">This compelling verse highlights that Yeshua urges us: if we seek eternal life, we must follow His commandments. See Leviticus 18:5, Ezekiel</note>women and children.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="20" osisRef="Matt.14.21" osisID="Matt.14.21!note.20">This word is first found in Mishnah Avodah Zara 5:3.</note><verse eID="Matt.14.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.22" osisID="Matt.14.22" n="22"/>Then quickly he commanded his talmidim (students) to ascend the ship and go before him to cross over the river, and sent away the communities.<verse eID="Matt.14.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.23" osisID="Matt.14.23" n="23"/>But when he sent away the community, he ascended the mountain by himself to pray.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="21" osisRef="Matt.14.23" osisID="Matt.14.23!note.21">Literally, “he/it lent.” It appears the author intended to write it as a noun, but wrote as a verb.</note><verse eID="Matt.14.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.24" osisID="Matt.14.24" n="24"/>So, he was alone, and the ship was far off from the land, and the sea was greatly raging from the waves, and the wind was against them.<verse eID="Matt.14.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.25" osisID="Matt.14.25" n="25"/>And at the fourth guard of the night, Yeshua came near them, strolling upon the sea.<verse eID="Matt.14.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.26" osisID="Matt.14.26" n="26"/>And his talmidim saw <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="22" osisRef="Matt.14.26" osisID="Matt.14.26!note.22">Literally, “And he saw, his talmidim…”</note>one walking on the sea. <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="23" osisRef="Matt.14.26" osisID="Matt.14.26!note.23">This appears to be a misspelled word and should be תִּלְּמְדו “you will learn.” It appears to be a defective spelling of “you (mp) will learn.”</note>So, they were amazed and said, “It is miraculous!” and from fear, they shouted.<verse eID="Matt.14.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.27" osisID="Matt.14.27" n="27"/>Then Yeshua said to them, “Harden your heart, it is I; you are to not fear!”<verse eID="Matt.14.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.28" osisID="Matt.14.28" n="28"/>Then Pedro (Peter) answered and said, “My Master, if it is you, command me to come to you<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="24" osisRef="Matt.14.28" osisID="Matt.14.28!note.24">Literally, “If it is you, command that I, he will come…” Thus,</note> to you on the water.”<verse eID="Matt.14.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.29" osisID="Matt.14.29" n="29"/>And Yeshua said to him, “Come!” And Pedro (Peter) descended from the ship and walked upon the sea and came beside Yeshua.<verse eID="Matt.14.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.30" osisID="Matt.14.30" n="30"/>And when a hard wind came, he feared and began to sink. Then he raised his voice and shouted, “My Lord, save me!”<verse eID="Matt.14.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.31" osisID="Matt.14.31" n="31"/>And in that hour, he extended his hand and said, “Because of what little faith ‒ you were with doubts!”<verse eID="Matt.14.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.32" osisID="Matt.14.32" n="32"/>But when they ascended the ship, the wind ceased.<verse eID="Matt.14.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.33" osisID="Matt.14.33" n="33"/>So, those in the ship came and bowed down to him and said, “In truth, you are the Son of Elohim (God).”<verse eID="Matt.14.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.34" osisID="Matt.14.34" n="34"/>Then they descended and came to the land of Gennosar (Gennesaret).<verse eID="Matt.14.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.35" osisID="Matt.14.35" n="35"/>When the men <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="28" osisRef="Matt.14.35" osisID="Matt.14.35!note.28">Can have a common plural meaning, “people.”</note>of that place knew him, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="29" osisRef="Matt.14.35" osisID="Matt.14.35!note.29">This word is first found in the Mishnah Tractate Sanhedrin 6:4.</note>they sent out to the city and its surroundings, and approached him with all those who were doing iniquity.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="30" osisRef="Matt.14.35" osisID="Matt.14.35!note.30">See footnote on Matthew 14:15 for more details on misspelled word.</note><verse eID="Matt.14.35"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.14.36" osisID="Matt.14.36" n="36"/>So they requested him that they may offer up <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="32" osisRef="Matt.14.36" osisID="Matt.14.36!note.32">Literally, “sacrifice their hand.”</note>their hand for the corners of his wings of his clothing. Then all who touched were healed.<verse eID="Matt.14.36"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.14"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.15" osisID="Matt.15" n="15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.1" osisID="Matt.15.1" n="1"/>Then the Pharisees and scribes from Yerushalayim (Jerusalem) approached Yeshua and said,<verse eID="Matt.15.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.2" osisID="Matt.15.2" n="2"/>“Why do your talmidim (students) transgress against the religious law <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="4" osisRef="Matt.15.2" osisID="Matt.15.2!note.4">The pronoun for “he” or “it” is an added pronoun as it is included in the word ענה (ana, “he/it answered).. It is possible the author intended to make it present tense.</note>of the sages, and not wash hands when they eat bread?”<verse eID="Matt.15.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.3" osisID="Matt.15.3" n="3"/>Yeshua answered and said before them, “For what reason do you transgress against the commandments of Yehovah for your pleasure!”<verse eID="Matt.15.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.4" osisID="Matt.15.4" n="4"/>Yehovah said, “Honor your father and your mother.” <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="6" osisRef="Matt.15.4" osisID="Matt.15.4!note.6">This verse is comparable to Yehovah speaking from Heaven in Matthew 3:17, and in the Cochin Matthew 3:17, it states, “...and to him,</note>And whoever curses them, shall surely die.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="7" osisRef="Matt.15.4" osisID="Matt.15.4!note.7">See Exodus 21:17 and Leviticus 20:9.</note><verse eID="Matt.15.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.5" osisID="Matt.15.5" n="5"/>You, what do you say? “All who say to their father and mother, ‘You drew near me to plot against me.’”<verse eID="Matt.15.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.6" osisID="Matt.15.6" n="6"/>And they do not honor their father and mother because of your enjoyment.<verse eID="Matt.15.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.7" osisID="Matt.15.7" n="7"/>Unbelievers! Plentily did Yeshayah (Isaiah) prophesy about you and said,<verse eID="Matt.15.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.8" osisID="Matt.15.8" n="8"/>“This people honor me with its mouth and lips, but its heart is distanced from me.”<verse eID="Matt.15.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.9" osisID="Matt.15.9" n="9"/>“And their fearing me when they study, even after this, is a commandment of men that is taught.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="13" osisRef="Matt.15.9" osisID="Matt.15.9!note.13">This is a harsh word that can mean “whore, fornicator,” or “prostitute.” The proper word for “adulterer” is נואף (no’ef).</note><verse eID="Matt.15.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.10" osisID="Matt.15.10" n="10"/>Then he called the community and said to them, “You are to hear and understand.”<verse eID="Matt.15.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.11" osisID="Matt.15.11" n="11"/>It is not all that enters into the mouth that defiles the sons of man, but all that goes out from the mouth that defiles the sons of man.<verse eID="Matt.15.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.12" osisID="Matt.15.12" n="12"/>Then the talmidim (students) approached him and said to him, “You should know that the Pharisees who heard this word became discontented.”<verse eID="Matt.15.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.13" osisID="Matt.15.13" n="13"/>He answered and said to them, “All plantings in a place that is not planted of my Father in heaven, will be uprooted.<verse eID="Matt.15.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.14" osisID="Matt.15.14" n="14"/>You are to leave them: they are blind and leading the blind, and the blind leaders of the blind, by necessity in a place of walking, for two of them fall into the pit.”<verse eID="Matt.15.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.15" osisID="Matt.15.15" n="15"/>Then, Pedro (Peter) answered and said, “My Master, interpret this word for us.”<verse eID="Matt.15.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.16" osisID="Matt.15.16" n="16"/>And then he <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="19" osisRef="Matt.15.16" osisID="Matt.15.16!note.19">This compelling verse highlights that Yeshua urges us: if we seek eternal life, we must follow His commandments. See Leviticus 18:5, Ezekiel</note>said, “Yet likewise, you haven’t become bearers of intelligence?”<verse eID="Matt.15.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.17" osisID="Matt.15.17" n="17"/>“Do you know that all of what goes to the mouth to the belly, it goes. And from there it is purified, and it goes outside?”<verse eID="Matt.15.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.18" osisID="Matt.15.18" n="18"/>“And all that goes out from the mouth, goes out from the heart, and it defiles sons of man.”<verse eID="Matt.15.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.19" osisID="Matt.15.19" n="19"/>“From the heart go out evil thoughts, and killing, and fornication, theft, adultery, lying witness, and defaming.”<verse eID="Matt.15.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.20" osisID="Matt.15.20" n="20"/>“These are those that defile sons of man, but whoever eats without handwashing is not defiled.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="20" osisRef="Matt.15.20" osisID="Matt.15.20!note.20">This word is first found in Mishnah Avodah Zara 5:3.</note><verse eID="Matt.15.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.21" osisID="Matt.15.21" n="21"/>Then Yeshua went out from there and came to the region of Tzor (Tyre) and Tzidon (Sidon).<verse eID="Matt.15.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.22" osisID="Matt.15.22" n="22"/>Then came a Canaanite woman from that region, shouting and crying, saying, “Have mercy on me, my Lord, son of David; my daughter is seized by a demon!”<verse eID="Matt.15.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.23" osisID="Matt.15.23" n="23"/>He did not respond any word, and his talmidim (students) approached and sought of him, and said, “Leave her, for she weeps after us!”<verse eID="Matt.15.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.24" osisID="Matt.15.24" n="24"/>He ‒ he answered and said to her,” I was not sent except to the lost sheep from the house of Yisrael (Israel).”<verse eID="Matt.15.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.25" osisID="Matt.15.25" n="25"/>In the hour she came, she wept, “My Master, help me!”<verse eID="Matt.15.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.26" osisID="Matt.15.26" n="26"/>Yeshua said to her, “It is not fitting for you to take a morsel of bread belonging to the children and give it to the dogs.”<verse eID="Matt.15.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.27" osisID="Matt.15.27" n="27"/>She ‒ she replied, “So, the dogs eat of the crumbs of bread that falls from the table of our masters, and they live.”<verse eID="Matt.15.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.28" osisID="Matt.15.28" n="28"/>Then Yeshua answered, “Woman, great is your faith; may it be done for you as you desired.” And her daughter was healed from that hour.<verse eID="Matt.15.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.29" osisID="Matt.15.29" n="29"/>And he left <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="26" osisRef="Matt.15.29" osisID="Matt.15.29!note.26">Aramaic word that is a metaphor for making it rain. See Aph Dem 4.65:25: ܐܪܛܡ ܬܚܢܘ.</note>there and came upon the southern sea of Galil (Galilee), and he ascended a mountain and sat there.<verse eID="Matt.15.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.30" osisID="Matt.15.30" n="30"/>Then approached him, all the communities, many who had with them, cripples, blind, deaf, and sickness of gout of the feet, and many others who fell at the feet of Yeshua, and he healed them.<verse eID="Matt.15.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.31" osisID="Matt.15.31" n="31"/>How astonished were the communities who saw a mute speaking, and one possessed of gout, healed, and the lame walking and the blind seeing. So, they praised the God of Yisrael (Israel)!<verse eID="Matt.15.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.32" osisID="Matt.15.32" n="32"/>Then he approached his talmidim (students) and said to them, “I have compassion on this community which these three days dwelled with me, and in case they did not have food, and I don’t want to send them away fasting, lest they become wearied on the way.<verse eID="Matt.15.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.33" osisID="Matt.15.33" n="33"/>His talmidim (students) said, "Where will bread come to us in this dry place to satisfy all this community?”<verse eID="Matt.15.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.34" osisID="Matt.15.34" n="34"/>He said to them, “How much bread do you have?” They said, “Seven loaves and a few small fish.”<verse eID="Matt.15.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.35" osisID="Matt.15.35" n="35"/>Then he commanded the community to sit on the earth.<verse eID="Matt.15.35"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.36" osisID="Matt.15.36" n="36"/>Then he took the seven bread and fish and blessed. Then he cut it for his disciples, and his disciples gave to the community.<verse eID="Matt.15.36"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.37" osisID="Matt.15.37" n="37"/>And all of them ate and were satisfied. And there remained of the crumbles, seven baskets.<verse eID="Matt.15.37"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.38" osisID="Matt.15.38" n="38"/>This who ate, there were four thousand men, apart from the little ones, and women.<verse eID="Matt.15.38"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.15.39" osisID="Matt.15.39" n="39"/>And when the community went away, he ascended the ship and came to the area of Megiddo.<verse eID="Matt.15.39"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.15"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.16" osisID="Matt.16" n="16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.1" osisID="Matt.16.1" n="1"/>And the Pharisees and Sadducees approached to test him and ask him for a sign from the heavens, shown to them.<verse eID="Matt.16.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.2" osisID="Matt.16.2" n="2"/>Then he answered, and he <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="4" osisRef="Matt.16.2" osisID="Matt.16.2!note.4">The pronoun for “he” or “it” is an added pronoun as it is included in the word ענה (ana, “he/it answered).. It is possible the author intended to make it present tense.</note>said to them, “When it is evening time, you say, ‘It is a good hour, because the heavens are red.’”<verse eID="Matt.16.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.3" osisID="Matt.16.3" n="3"/>“And in the morning you say, ‘It is a cold day, and the heavens were red in color.’ Unbelievers! Do you know he said to examine the signs of the heavens, but even a mark of time, you do not know how to differentiate?”<verse eID="Matt.16.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.4" osisID="Matt.16.4" n="4"/>An evil and stubborn tribe! You seek a sign, but a sign is not given, but of Yonah (Jonah), the Prophet.” And he left them and went away.<verse eID="Matt.16.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.5" osisID="Matt.16.5" n="5"/>And when he came with his disciples to pass over that side, they forgot to take bread with them.<verse eID="Matt.16.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.6" osisID="Matt.16.6" n="6"/>He said this to them, “Beware of the tricks of the Pharisees and Sadducees.”<verse eID="Matt.16.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.7" osisID="Matt.16.7" n="7"/>And they were comforted in their heart and said, “Why is all this, since we did not take bread?”<verse eID="Matt.16.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.8" osisID="Matt.16.8" n="8"/>And Yeshua, when he understood, said to them, “Why are you thinking in your heart, who are little of faith, regarding the bread you did not bring?”<verse eID="Matt.16.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.9" osisID="Matt.16.9" n="9"/>“Until now, you did not understand, nor know those five breads for the five thousand, and how many baskets you took away?”<verse eID="Matt.16.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.10" osisID="Matt.16.10" n="10"/>“And even those seven bread for the four thousand, and how many baskets you took away?”<verse eID="Matt.16.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.11" osisID="Matt.16.11" n="11"/>“Truth, you did not understand that I told you about the bread? Rather, you should beware of the tricks<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="9" osisRef="Matt.16.11" osisID="Matt.16.11!note.9">Or may also mean, “against you.” It also appears that the scribe may not have intended to write the word אני (ani, “I”), with the middle letter being the letter nun, but instead, he may have intended to write the word אוי (oy, “Woe!), with the middle letter being a vav. Thus, if</note> of the Pharisees and Sadducees.”<verse eID="Matt.16.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.12" osisID="Matt.16.12" n="12"/>After this, they understood that he did not say to beware of the bread, but of the Talmud (teaching) of the Pharisees and Sadducees.<verse eID="Matt.16.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.13" osisID="Matt.16.13" n="13"/>When Yeshua came to the land of Caesar of Phillippos (Philip), he asked his talmidim and said, “What do the sons of adam say about me, if I am a son of a man?”<verse eID="Matt.16.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.14" osisID="Matt.16.14" n="14"/>They replied to him and said, “There are some saying he is Yochanan (John) the Converter, and some say Eliyyah (Elijah), and others say Yirmeyah (Jeremiah), or one of the prophets.<verse eID="Matt.16.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.15" osisID="Matt.16.15" n="15"/>He said to them, “What do you say about me? Who am I?”<verse eID="Matt.16.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.16" osisID="Matt.16.16" n="16"/>Pedro (Peter) replied and said to him, “You are the Messiah, Son of the Living Elohim!”<verse eID="Matt.16.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.17" osisID="Matt.16.17" n="17"/>Yeshua answered and said, “Good for you, Pedro (Peter), son of Yonah (Jonah), for flesh and blood has not revealed it to you, but my Father in the heavens!”<verse eID="Matt.16.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.18" osisID="Matt.16.18" n="18"/>I also say to you that you are Pedro (Peter), for on this stone, I build an assembly (knesset); and the gates of Gehinnom will not inherit it.<verse eID="Matt.16.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.19" osisID="Matt.16.19" n="19"/>To you <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="12" osisRef="Matt.16.19" osisID="Matt.16.19!note.12">Literal meanings: “drives out, thrusts, expels.”</note>I will give the keys of heaven and all of what you prohibit <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="13" osisRef="Matt.16.19" osisID="Matt.16.19!note.13">This is a harsh word that can mean “whore, fornicator,” or “prostitute.” The proper word for “adulterer” is נואף (no’ef).</note>in the world will be prohibited in heaven, and all of what remains<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="14" osisRef="Matt.16.19" osisID="Matt.16.19!note.14">Collectively, including details from Matthew 5:32 and Luke 16:18, along with this passage, the man who marries the first wife will be an adulterer נואף (no’ef) because the woman was not divorced properly with her כריתות (keritut, “divorcement”). The woman who is separated, or rather “put away” גרושה (gerusha), is still considered married. The man in Matthew 19:9 makes himself a fornicator זונה (zonah) by taking a 2nd woman without</note> will be permitted in the heavens.<verse eID="Matt.16.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.20" osisID="Matt.16.20" n="20"/>After this, he commanded his talmidim (students) that they shall not say the son of man that he is the Messiah.<verse eID="Matt.16.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.21" osisID="Matt.16.21" n="21"/>And from that hour, Yeshua began to hint to his talmidim (students) that he would get ready to go up to Jerusalem and greatly quarrel with the elders, the priests, and the scribes, and that they would kill him, but on the third day he would arise.<verse eID="Matt.16.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.22" osisID="Matt.16.22" n="22"/>And Pedro (Peter) spoke and said, “God forbid that this evil be to you, my Master.”<verse eID="Matt.16.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.23" osisID="Matt.16.23" n="23"/>Then he responded and said to Pedro (Peter), “Go you! Get behind me, evil inclination (Yetzer ha-Ra)! Already, you do not have words like children of Elohim (God), but like sons of man.”<verse eID="Matt.16.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.24" osisID="Matt.16.24" n="24"/>Then Yeshua said to his disciples, “All who want to come after me will give up his soul to death and come after me.”<verse eID="Matt.16.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.25" osisID="Matt.16.25" n="25"/>For all who want a restful spirit for his soul, even he will lose it on my account, and all who destroy his own soul for me will rest.<verse eID="Matt.16.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.26" osisID="Matt.16.26" n="26"/>“All who purchase one man, it is as if he bought all the world, but while reducing his own soul in death ‒ or will give him a son of man as atonement for his soul?”<verse eID="Matt.16.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.27" osisID="Matt.16.27" n="27"/>The Son of man is about to come in the excellencies of his Father with the holy angels, and then he will repay all of man and woman according to their works.<verse eID="Matt.16.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.16.28" osisID="Matt.16.28" n="28"/>“Truth I say to you, that there are people sitting here who will not taste death until they see the Son of Man about to go in his kingdom.”<verse eID="Matt.16.28"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.16"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.17" osisID="Matt.17" n="17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.1" osisID="Matt.17.1" n="1"/>And after six days <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="4" osisRef="Matt.17.1" osisID="Matt.17.1!note.4">The pronoun for “he” or “it” is an added pronoun as it is included in the word ענה (ana, “he/it answered).. It is possible the author intended to make it present tense.</note>Yeshua took Pedro (Peter) and Ya’akov (Jacob), and Yochanan (John), his brother, and caused them to ascend alone.<verse eID="Matt.17.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.2" osisID="Matt.17.2" n="2"/>And Yeshua changed his appearance and his face shone like the sun, and his clothing was white like radiance.<verse eID="Matt.17.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.3" osisID="Matt.17.3" n="3"/>And it resembled to them there, the faces of Moses and Elijah, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="5" osisRef="Matt.17.3" osisID="Matt.17.3!note.5">Genesis 1:27.</note>who were speaking with him.<verse eID="Matt.17.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.4" osisID="Matt.17.4" n="4"/>Pedro (Peter) answered and said to Yeshua, “My Lord, that we should sit here, and if it is acceptable in your eyes, let us make three places: One for you and one for Moses, and one for Elijah.”<verse eID="Matt.17.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.5" osisID="Matt.17.5" n="5"/>And when he was speaking, there was a bright cloud and a shadow over them, and a voice came out from the cloud, “Behold my beloved son, and in him I am satisfied, and to him, you will listen.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="6" osisRef="Matt.17.5" osisID="Matt.17.5!note.6">This verse is comparable to Yehovah speaking from Heaven in Matthew 3:17, and in the Cochin Matthew 3:17, it states, “...and to him,</note><verse eID="Matt.17.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.6" osisID="Matt.17.6" n="6"/>And when the talmidim (students) heard, they fell upon their faces and greatly feared.<verse eID="Matt.17.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.7" osisID="Matt.17.7" n="7"/>And Yeshua approached them and said to them, “Arise, and do not fear!”<verse eID="Matt.17.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.8" osisID="Matt.17.8" n="8"/>And they opened their eyes and did not see any man except for Yeshua alone.<verse eID="Matt.17.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.9" osisID="Matt.17.9" n="9"/>And when they descended from the mountain, Yeshua ordered and said to them, “Regarding my aspects<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="7" osisRef="Matt.17.9" osisID="Matt.17.9!note.7">See Exodus 21:17 and Leviticus 20:9.</note> of the Son of man's vision until the Son of man is risen from the dead.”<verse eID="Matt.17.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.10" osisID="Matt.17.10" n="10"/>And his talmidim (students) asked and said to him, “The scribes say that Eliyyah (Elijah) will come first.”<verse eID="Matt.17.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.11" osisID="Matt.17.11" n="11"/>Yeshua answered and said to them, “Elyyah (Elijah) will come first to whoever acknowledges in him, he will be found.”<verse eID="Matt.17.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.12" osisID="Matt.17.12" n="12"/>I say to you, Eliyyah (Elijah) came and you did not know him, and they did with him all that they wanted: likewise, the Son of man is about to be struck by them.”<verse eID="Matt.17.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.13" osisID="Matt.17.13" n="13"/>And then the talmidim (students) understood that he was speaking to them about Yochanan (John) the purifier.<verse eID="Matt.17.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.14" osisID="Matt.17.14" n="14"/>Then, when they had come to the house of their community, and one man drew near to him and bowed down upon his knees and said to him,<verse eID="Matt.17.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.15" osisID="Matt.17.15" n="15"/>“My Lord have mercy upon my son; he has become greatly mad, because sometimes he falls in the water and other times in the fire.”<verse eID="Matt.17.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.16" osisID="Matt.17.16" n="16"/>“So I brought him to your talmidim (students), but they were not able to heal him.”<verse eID="Matt.17.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.17" osisID="Matt.17.17" n="17"/>Yeshua answered and said, “I am toward <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="9" osisRef="Matt.17.17" osisID="Matt.17.17!note.9">Or may also mean, “against you.” It also appears that the scribe may not have intended to write the word אני (ani, “I”), with the middle letter being the letter nun, but instead, he may have intended to write the word אוי (oy, “Woe!), with the middle letter being a vav. Thus, if</note>you, lowly generation, who does not believe. Until when do you stray? And until when must you be taught by me? Bring him here!”<verse eID="Matt.17.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.18" osisID="Matt.17.18" n="18"/>And Yeshua rebuked and expelled it to come out of him. And the child was healed in that hour.<verse eID="Matt.17.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.19" osisID="Matt.17.19" n="19"/>In that hour, his talmidim (students) drew near Yeshua and said to him, “Why were we not able to heal him?”<verse eID="Matt.17.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.20" osisID="Matt.17.20" n="20"/>Yeshua said to them, “Because you did not believe well. Truth I say to you, that if there was faith in you as a lone mustard, you could even say to the mountain that it will change its place, and it would be changed. And additionally, they will not lack of anything.<verse eID="Matt.17.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.21" osisID="Matt.17.21" n="21"/>Behold, this generation will not go out but if by prayer and in fasting.<verse eID="Matt.17.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.22" osisID="Matt.17.22" n="22"/>When they were turning into Galil (Galilee), Yeshua said to them, “The Son of man is about to fall into the hand of a son of a man,” and they will kill him, and on the third day, he will rise.” And they were greatly grieved. And when he came to Kephar Nahum (Capernaum), the tax collectors, who take two by two zuzin-coins<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="15" osisRef="Matt.17.22" osisID="Matt.17.22!note.15">Can also be translated to, “men with a woman.”</note> for no sum,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="16" osisRef="Matt.17.22" osisID="Matt.17.22!note.16">In Matthew 16:19, Yeshua is speaking directly to Peter about building an assembly upon the stone of truth that Yeshua is the Messiah by saying, “To you I will give the keys of heaven and all of what you prohibit ( אוסר oser) in the world will be prohibited in the heavens, and all of what remains will be permitted in the heaven.” However, in Matthew 18:18, Yeshua is speaking directly to all the disciples about handling conflict in the community by saying, “...all what is bound ( קשור kishur) on earth, is bound in the heavens: and what is opened on earth will be opened in the heavens.” These are speaking of two different truths: 1. The truth that Yeshua is the Messiah is the foundation of the new covenant assembly. However, if “the heavens” is an idiom of “heavenly beings,” according to Joseph A. Fitzmeyer’s analysis of the 1350 Hittite treaty (See the English Edition of the Aramaic text, with references to biblical parallels, in Joseph A. Fitzmyer, trans., “The Inscriptions of Bar-Ga’yah and Mati’el from Sefire,” The Context of Scripture 2.82 (W. Hallo and K. L. Younger, eds. [Leiden: Brill, 2000]), 213–215), then the keys to the heavens appear to be keys that permit or prohibit heavenly beings in situations such as when an individual is turned over the satan for destruction of the flesh and correction when Paul turned two individuals over to Satan in 1 Corinthians 5:5, and 1 Timothy 1:20. Even though Yeshua was speaking directly to Peter, and Paul, who was not a disciple of Yeshua but was sent by Yeshua, was given LEGAL AUTHORITY over the assemblies was able to turn these men over to Satan. This can also be done by those who have legal authority over the assembly, but it is recommended that you ask Yehovah to fight the fight on their behalf, other than attempting to face off with demonic entities. 2. In Matthew 18:18, the disciples are taught how to resolve conflicts and to be humble with a forgiving heart. This verse seems to indicate that “all what is bound,” meaning either by man or God, is bound in the heavens. Likewise, all that is opened on earth, by man or God, is opened in the heavens. Therefore, if a person “binds” something on earth, such as a spiritual being, then it is bound in the heavens. But if something is “opened” on earth — like a modern television, radio, internet, or a cursed item — then it is opened upon the</note> came before Pedro (Peter) and said to him, “Does not your Lord not pay the choicest money, his two zuzim-coins?”<verse eID="Matt.17.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.25" osisID="Matt.17.25" n="25"/>He said to them, “Good.” And when he (Peter) came near Yeshua to the house, Yeshua said to him, “What do you think, Pedro (Peter)? In the kingdom of this earth, from those who collect the tax, do they first collect from their children or from strangers?”<verse eID="Matt.17.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.26" osisID="Matt.17.26" n="26"/>Pedro (Peter) said to him, “From strangers, they first collect.” Yeshua said to him, “If so, the children will be children of freedom.”<verse eID="Matt.17.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.17.27" osisID="Matt.17.27" n="27"/>But so not to cause them to stumble, go to the sea and cast your fork into the water, and the fish that comes out first, you will open its mouth and find one stater-coin,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="18" osisRef="Matt.17.27" osisID="Matt.17.27!note.18">The Nit’pael verb binyan is a mix of the Nif’al and Hit’pael verb binyanim and was used regularly in Mishnaic Hebrew to express a</note> and take it and give on behalf of me and for you.<verse eID="Matt.17.27"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.17"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.18" osisID="Matt.18" n="18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.1" osisID="Matt.18.1" n="1"/>In that hour, the talmidim (students) approached Yeshua, and said, “Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?”<verse eID="Matt.18.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.2" osisID="Matt.18.2" n="2"/>Then Yeshua called for the boy, and he stood in their midst.<verse eID="Matt.18.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.3" osisID="Matt.18.3" n="3"/>And He said, “Truth I say that if you do not repent and make yourselves like children, you will not enter into the kingdom of heaven.”<verse eID="Matt.18.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.4" osisID="Matt.18.4" n="4"/>“He who humbles himself like this boy, he will be great in the kingdom of heaven.”<verse eID="Matt.18.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.5" osisID="Matt.18.5" n="5"/>“And whoever receives the boy in my name, he receives Me,”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="4" osisRef="Matt.18.5" osisID="Matt.18.5!note.4">The pronoun for “he” or “it” is an added pronoun as it is included in the word ענה (ana, “he/it answered).. It is possible the author intended to make it present tense.</note><verse eID="Matt.18.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.6" osisID="Matt.18.6" n="6"/>“and all who become stumbling blocks for children who believe in me, it is better for him to be given millstones that are hung by his neck and drown himself in the sea.”<verse eID="Matt.18.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.7" osisID="Matt.18.7" n="7"/>“Woe to the world because of those attacks! For there is about to come to him a stumbling block. Woe to the man for whose sake the attacks will come!”<verse eID="Matt.18.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.8" osisID="Matt.18.8" n="8"/>“If your hand or your foot snares you, cut it off and throw it from you, for it is better for you to come to life lame or left-handed<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="7" osisRef="Matt.18.8" osisID="Matt.18.8!note.7">See Exodus 21:17 and Leviticus 20:9.</note> and not with two feet and two hands into the fire and into Gehinnom.”<verse eID="Matt.18.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.9" osisID="Matt.18.9" n="9"/>“And if your eye causes you to fail, you should pluck it out and throw it out; it is better to enter with one eye into life than two eyes into Gehinnom.”<verse eID="Matt.18.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.10" osisID="Matt.18.10" n="10"/>“And you will see not to despise even one of the little ones: I say for the angels are seen all the time before my Father in heaven.”<verse eID="Matt.18.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.11" osisID="Matt.18.11" n="11"/>“Because I, the Son of man, redeem all those who are lost.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="10" osisRef="Matt.18.11" osisID="Matt.18.11!note.10">Deuteronomy 24:1.</note><verse eID="Matt.18.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.12" osisID="Matt.18.12" n="12"/>And from this, you will see: if there are sons of man that have a hundred sheep, and one who was lost, would they not leave remaining the ninety-nine, and intensely seek for the one that was lost?<verse eID="Matt.18.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.13" osisID="Matt.18.13" n="13"/>“And if it is found, truth, I say to you, there will be with this one, more joy than from the ninety-nine that were not lost.”<verse eID="Matt.18.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.14" osisID="Matt.18.14" n="14"/>“Is there not this same desire with your Father <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="13" osisRef="Matt.18.14" osisID="Matt.18.14!note.13">This is a harsh word that can mean “whore, fornicator,” or “prostitute.” The proper word for “adulterer” is נואף (no’ef).</note>in the heavens on losing of one of these little ones?”<verse eID="Matt.18.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.15" osisID="Matt.18.15" n="15"/>“If one of your brothers sinned against you, go and make peace <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="14" osisRef="Matt.18.15" osisID="Matt.18.15!note.14">Collectively, including details from Matthew 5:32 and Luke 16:18, along with this passage, the man who marries the first wife will be an adulterer נואף (no’ef) because the woman was not divorced properly with her כריתות (keritut, “divorcement”). The woman who is separated, or rather “put away” גרושה (gerusha), is still considered married. The man in Matthew 19:9 makes himself a fornicator זונה (zonah) by taking a 2nd woman without</note>between you and him, and if he has heard you, you have gained your brother.”<verse eID="Matt.18.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.16" osisID="Matt.18.16" n="16"/>“And if he will not hear, take two or three witnesses so that on the mouth of two witnesses, or by the mouth of three witnesses, every word will be established.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="15" osisRef="Matt.18.16" osisID="Matt.18.16!note.15">Can also be translated to, “men with a woman.”</note><verse eID="Matt.18.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.17" osisID="Matt.18.17" n="17"/>“And if this one does not hear, even to them, you are to tell the assembly, and if to the assembly, likewise, he does not hear, then he is to you as vile and despised.”<verse eID="Matt.18.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.18" osisID="Matt.18.18" n="18"/>“And truth I say to you, all what is bound on earth, will be bound in the heavens: and what is opened on earth will be opened in the heavens.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="16" osisRef="Matt.18.18" osisID="Matt.18.18!note.16">In Matthew 16:19, Yeshua is speaking directly to Peter about building an assembly upon the stone of truth that Yeshua is the Messiah by saying, “To you I will give the keys of heaven and all of what you prohibit ( אוסר oser) in the world will be prohibited in the heavens, and all of what remains will be permitted in the heaven.” However, in Matthew 18:18, Yeshua is speaking directly to all the disciples about handling conflict in the community by saying, “...all what is bound ( קשור kishur) on earth, is bound in the heavens: and what is opened on earth will be opened in the heavens.” These are speaking of two different truths: 1. The truth that Yeshua is the Messiah is the foundation of the new covenant assembly. However, if “the heavens” is an idiom of “heavenly beings,” according to Joseph A. Fitzmeyer’s analysis of the 1350 Hittite treaty (See the English Edition of the Aramaic text, with references to biblical parallels, in Joseph A. Fitzmyer, trans., “The Inscriptions of Bar-Ga’yah and Mati’el from Sefire,” The Context of Scripture 2.82 (W. Hallo and K. L. Younger, eds. [Leiden: Brill, 2000]), 213–215), then the keys to the heavens appear to be keys that permit or prohibit heavenly beings in situations such as when an individual is turned over the satan for destruction of the flesh and correction when Paul turned two individuals over to Satan in 1 Corinthians 5:5, and 1 Timothy 1:20. Even though Yeshua was speaking directly to Peter, and Paul, who was not a disciple of Yeshua but was sent by Yeshua, was given LEGAL AUTHORITY over the assemblies was able to turn these men over to Satan. This can also be done by those who have legal authority over the assembly, but it is recommended that you ask Yehovah to fight the fight on their behalf, other than attempting to face off with demonic entities. 2. In Matthew 18:18, the disciples are taught how to resolve conflicts and to be humble with a forgiving heart. This verse seems to indicate that “all what is bound,” meaning either by man or God, is bound in the heavens. Likewise, all that is opened on earth, by man or God, is opened in the heavens. Therefore, if a person “binds” something on earth, such as a spiritual being, then it is bound in the heavens. But if something is “opened” on earth — like a modern television, radio, internet, or a cursed item — then it is opened upon the</note><verse eID="Matt.18.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.19" osisID="Matt.18.19" n="19"/>“Again, I say to you, ‘If two of you desire one thing on this earth, regarding all you desire that you (intensely)<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="17" osisRef="Matt.18.19" osisID="Matt.18.19!note.17">See Isaiah 6:9–10.</note> ask, it will be for you by my Father who is in the heavens.’”<verse eID="Matt.18.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.20" osisID="Matt.18.20" n="20"/>“And in the place that two or three are assembled together for my name, I will be amongst them.”<verse eID="Matt.18.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.21" osisID="Matt.18.21" n="21"/>Then Pedro (Peter) approached him and said to him, “My Master, how many times if my brother sins against me, am I to forgive him? Up to seven times?”<verse eID="Matt.18.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.22" osisID="Matt.18.22" n="22"/>Yeshua said to him, “I have not said seven times, but seventy times seven, seven.”<verse eID="Matt.18.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.23" osisID="Matt.18.23" n="23"/>“Because the kingdom of heaven is likened to one king who wanted to take account of his servants,”<verse eID="Matt.18.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.24" osisID="Matt.18.24" n="24"/>“and when taking account, there was one debtor who was in debt of ten thousand talents.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="19" osisRef="Matt.18.24" osisID="Matt.18.24!note.19">This compelling verse highlights that Yeshua urges us: if we seek eternal life, we must follow His commandments. See Leviticus 18:5, Ezekiel</note><verse eID="Matt.18.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.25" osisID="Matt.18.25" n="25"/>“And when he was not able to pay it off, the master ordered him that he would sell his wife and children, and whatever he had entirely, and to pay.”<verse eID="Matt.18.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.26" osisID="Matt.18.26" n="26"/>Then he fell and bowed down and said, “My Master, be long-suffering (of your anger) and I will pay all of that there is to you.<verse eID="Matt.18.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.27" osisID="Matt.18.27" n="27"/>“And his master pitied him and released him and even forgave his debt.”<verse eID="Matt.18.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.28" osisID="Matt.18.28" n="28"/>This servant went out and found one man who was a debtor to him a hundred, and he seized him and strangled him and said, “You will pay me my debt!”<verse eID="Matt.18.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.29" osisID="Matt.18.29" n="29"/>And the debtor <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="21" osisRef="Matt.18.29" osisID="Matt.18.29!note.21">Literally, “he/it lent.” It appears the author intended to write it as a noun, but wrote as a verb.</note>fell at his foot and begged, “You should be patient <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="22" osisRef="Matt.18.29" osisID="Matt.18.29!note.22">Literally, “And he saw, his talmidim…”</note>and I will pay it off.”<verse eID="Matt.18.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.30" osisID="Matt.18.30" n="30"/>And this one did not want to, but he went and left him in the house of prisoners until he repaid the debt.<verse eID="Matt.18.30"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.31" osisID="Matt.18.31" n="31"/>When he saw this, their friends were greatly furious and came and informed their Lord about what had happened.<verse eID="Matt.18.31"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.32" osisID="Matt.18.32" n="32"/>The master called to him and said to him, “Wicked servant, how of all the debts I did forgive you because of your request!”<verse eID="Matt.18.32"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.33" osisID="Matt.18.33" n="33"/>“And should you not have mercy upon your friend like I had mercy over you?”<verse eID="Matt.18.33"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.34" osisID="Matt.18.34" n="34"/>And the master was angry and condemned him to beatings until he would pay off all his debt.<verse eID="Matt.18.34"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.18.35" osisID="Matt.18.35" n="35"/>“Thus does my Father do to you, if you do not forgive and pardon every man, their brother, from a willing heart.”<verse eID="Matt.18.35"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.18"/>
        <chapter sID="Matt.19" osisID="Matt.19" n="19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.1" osisID="Matt.19.1" n="1"/>And when these words of Yeshua’s were completed, he journeyed from Galil (Galillee) and came to the area of Yehudim (Jews) across the Yarden (Jordan).<verse eID="Matt.19.1"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.2" osisID="Matt.19.2" n="2"/>Then a great community went after him, and he healed them there.<verse eID="Matt.19.2"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.3" osisID="Matt.19.3" n="3"/>And the Pharisees approached him and were testing him and saying to him, “Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for every accusation?”<verse eID="Matt.19.3"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.4" osisID="Matt.19.4" n="4"/>He <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="4" osisRef="Matt.19.4" osisID="Matt.19.4!note.4">The pronoun for “he” or “it” is an added pronoun as it is included in the word ענה (ana, “he/it answered).. It is possible the author intended to make it present tense.</note>answers and says to them, “Did you not read that He who made them, 'created them,' in the beginning?<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="5" osisRef="Matt.19.4" osisID="Matt.19.4!note.5">Genesis 1:27.</note><verse eID="Matt.19.4"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.5" osisID="Matt.19.5" n="5"/>And he said, “Because of this, leave his father and his mother, and cleave to his wife, and they will become one flesh.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="7" osisRef="Matt.19.5" osisID="Matt.19.5!note.7">See Exodus 21:17 and Leviticus 20:9.</note><verse eID="Matt.19.5"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.6" osisID="Matt.19.6" n="6"/>“From this, it is seen, it is not two, but one body: Because thus, Elohim (God) made a pair of human beings, that he would not divorce."<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="8" osisRef="Matt.19.6" osisID="Matt.19.6!note.8">This word for “divorce” ( יגרש - yegaresh) describes one who “drives out, thrusts out, or expels” his wife.</note><verse eID="Matt.19.6"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.7" osisID="Matt.19.7" n="7"/>He said to him, “Why did Moses do it this way? That he commanded to give a writing of divorce and release.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="10" osisRef="Matt.19.7" osisID="Matt.19.7!note.10">Deuteronomy 24:1.</note><verse eID="Matt.19.7"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.8" osisID="Matt.19.8" n="8"/>And he said to them, “Mosheh (Moses), for heaviness of heart, that you have, allowed for you to divorce your wives, but from the beginning, it was not so.”<verse eID="Matt.19.8"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.9" osisID="Matt.19.9" n="9"/>“I say to you, whoever sends away <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="12" osisRef="Matt.19.9" osisID="Matt.19.9!note.12">Literal meanings: “drives out, thrusts, expels.”</note>his wife without her being a fornicator <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="13" osisRef="Matt.19.9" osisID="Matt.19.9!note.13">This is a harsh word that can mean “whore, fornicator,” or “prostitute.” The proper word for “adulterer” is נואף (no’ef).</note>and takes another wife, commits fornication: and whoever that marries her (i.e., the first wife),<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="14" osisRef="Matt.19.9" osisID="Matt.19.9!note.14">Collectively, including details from Matthew 5:32 and Luke 16:18, along with this passage, the man who marries the first wife will be an adulterer נואף (no’ef) because the woman was not divorced properly with her כריתות (keritut, “divorcement”). The woman who is separated, or rather “put away” גרושה (gerusha), is still considered married. The man in Matthew 19:9 makes himself a fornicator זונה (zonah) by taking a 2nd woman without</note> commits fornication.”<verse eID="Matt.19.9"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.10" osisID="Matt.19.10" n="10"/>His talmidim (students) said to him, “If such is the case between husbands with a wife, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="15" osisRef="Matt.19.10" osisID="Matt.19.10!note.15">Can also be translated to, “men with a woman.”</note>then he will not be able to marry a wife.”<verse eID="Matt.19.10"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.11" osisID="Matt.19.11" n="11"/>He said to them, “Not every man is able to learn this, except whoever it is given to.”<verse eID="Matt.19.11"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.12" osisID="Matt.19.12" n="12"/>There is whomever that has faith from the womb of his mother, and is born, there is whomever that has faith, it is only his faith by man. And there are some who have faith who did by their own will in faith for the sake of the kingdom of heaven. And whoever wills to succeed, is able to succeed.”<verse eID="Matt.19.12"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.13" osisID="Matt.19.13" n="13"/>After this, they brought children to him, so that he may place his hand upon them to bless them, but his talmidim (students) were not willing.<verse eID="Matt.19.13"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.14" osisID="Matt.19.14" n="14"/>Then Yeshua said, “Allow the children to come to me, and do not withhold them, for they are little ones, but they will be in the kingdom of heaven.”<verse eID="Matt.19.14"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.15" osisID="Matt.19.15" n="15"/>And he placed his hand upon them, and went from there.<verse eID="Matt.19.15"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.16" osisID="Matt.19.16" n="16"/>And one approached him and said to him, “Good Teacher, what good thing should I do that I may have eternal life?”<verse eID="Matt.19.16"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.17" osisID="Matt.19.17" n="17"/>And he said to him, “Why do you call me good? There is none good except One. And if you want to live forever, keep His commandments.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="19" osisRef="Matt.19.17" osisID="Matt.19.17!note.19">This compelling verse highlights that Yeshua urges us: if we seek eternal life, we must follow His commandments. See Leviticus 18:5, Ezekiel</note><verse eID="Matt.19.17"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.18" osisID="Matt.19.18" n="18"/>And he said to him. Behold! These are the commandments that Yeshua said, “You shall not murder, nor shall you commit adultery, nor shall you steal, nor shall you bear false witness against your neighbor,”<verse eID="Matt.19.18"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.19" osisID="Matt.19.19" n="19"/>“honor your father and mother, and you shall love your neighbor as yourself.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="20" osisRef="Matt.19.19" osisID="Matt.19.19!note.20">This word is first found in Mishnah Avodah Zara 5:3.</note><verse eID="Matt.19.19"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.20" osisID="Matt.19.20" n="20"/>He said to him, “Since I was a young man in my adolescence, I kept all these. Even so, what am I lacking the most?<verse eID="Matt.19.20"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.21" osisID="Matt.19.21" n="21"/>Yeshua said to them, “If you want to be complete, sell your inheritance and give it to the poor, and it will be there for you in heaven; then come follow me.”<verse eID="Matt.19.21"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.22" osisID="Matt.19.22" n="22"/>And when this young man heard these words, he went away in sorrow, for he had much property.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="21" osisRef="Matt.19.22" osisID="Matt.19.22!note.21">Literally, “he/it lent.” It appears the author intended to write it as a noun, but wrote as a verb.</note><verse eID="Matt.19.22"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.23" osisID="Matt.19.23" n="23"/>Yeshua said this to his students (talmidim), “Truth I say to you, the rich are yet to ascend to the heavens.”<verse eID="Matt.19.23"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.24" osisID="Matt.19.24" n="24"/>I, I say, as it will be much easier for that camel to enter the small hole of a needle than for the rich to enter the kingdom of heaven.”<verse eID="Matt.19.24"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.25" osisID="Matt.19.25" n="25"/>The talmidim (students), when they heard, were much amazed and said with amazement of heart, “Who is able to be delivered accordingly for eternal life?”<verse eID="Matt.19.25"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.26" osisID="Matt.19.26" n="26"/>Yeshua shouted at them and said to them in anger, “It is not possible for the sons of man: with Elohim (God), all what they seek, they will find.”<verse eID="Matt.19.26"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.27" osisID="Matt.19.27" n="27"/>Then Pedro (Peter) answered, and said to him,” Behold, we left all we had and went after you. Why will it not be so for us?”<verse eID="Matt.19.27"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.28" osisID="Matt.19.28" n="28"/>Yeshua said to them, “In truth, I say to you that you, who follow me in this world, that like this we sit on the throne of the kingdom, therefore you will also sit upon twelve thrones and judge over the twelve tribes of Israel.”<verse eID="Matt.19.28"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.29" osisID="Matt.19.29" n="29"/>“And everyone <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="23" osisRef="Matt.19.29" osisID="Matt.19.29!note.23">This appears to be a misspelled word and should be תִּלְּמְדו “you will learn.” It appears to be a defective spelling of “you (mp) will learn.”</note>who leaves his household, or his brothers, or his sister, or his father, or his mother, or his wife, or his children, or relatives for my name, in truth, they will receive their reward and live for eternal life.”<verse eID="Matt.19.29"/>
        <verse sID="Matt.19.30" osisID="Matt.19.30" n="30"/>Many of those who were first <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="25" osisRef="Matt.19.30" osisID="Matt.19.30!note.25">The word קדמונים is the Hebrew equivalent of the Aramaic ܐܝܡܕܩ (qaddimāya), meaning the “first ones” from the ancient world. While accurately</note>became last, and the last became first.<verse eID="Matt.19.30"/>
        <chapter eID="Matt.19"/>
      </div>
  </osisText>
</osis>
